      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS,</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS,</p>
        <p>A NOTE OF ADMIRATION. —A love-letter. NOT TO BE SNEEZED AT.—An empty snuff-box. THE LAST APPEAL. —A shoemakers' strike. THE worst wheel of the cart always makes most creaking. THE. reason why egotists find the world so ugly is because they only see themselves in it. • THE wind does not behave well in winter, but in spring and summer it " turns over a new leaf." WE often hear of a man "being in advance of his age," but who ever heard of a woman being in the same predicament? HE: "so the engagement is at an end?" She: "Yes." He : " Who broke it ?" She: "The minister who married us." AUNTY: "Why did you laugh when you told me your dear little dog was dead?" Lit!le Dick (ehok- ingly) : "So 's to keep from cryin'." VISITOR, AT SEASII,E : "And what sort of people do you get down here ?" Old Salt : " Oh, all sorts —some of 'em very stylish fol, and some not very much better than yourself.' "Oult mamma is very kind to us. Every time we drink our cod-liver oil without crying we get fid. each." "And what do you do with the money ?" " Mamma buys seine more oil with it." THE DIFFEBENCE.—Young gir s like balls, 2,soung men like belles, brokers like bills, topers like bowls, and cardinals like bulls. By just changing a vowel, all tastes are suited. TtACHEII. : " You don't advance at all, Smith. Why, when I was your age I wade my mark." Smith : " What, sir ! Con diet you write, theur CoLmNs once wrote: "A governess ia a lady who is not iich, and a duchess is a lady who is not poor. That is all the ditierence I acknow- ledge between them." Doctor: "Well, Pat, have you taken the box oh pills sent you?" " Pat : "Yes, sir ; but I don't feel any better. Maybe the lid hasn't come off , yet l" "IN literature there seems to be a man con . stantly catching our ideas," said a writer. "Yes, someone rejoined ; "and the trouble is that catches them before you do I" : "Those are pretty tough-looking ' .patent leather shoes you insve on." Garner : "They were all right originally, but the pat at has expired on them." Wnaa, I am glad that P.osalle's tastes are _literary, and she is going to marry a man of letters." " Yes, she's going to wed a sign. painter." A BUNG from 3 beer barrel blew out and in- stantly killed a Harrisburg man who stood in its way. The coroner can hardy escape the , verdict that the man died from the effects of , liquor. " liatwioNs is very fond of his horse, isn't he ?" "Why, no ; he hates him." "That's queer. I saw him riding in the Park the other day, and he had his arms about the animal's neck." FOND Hunnv : " Why that deep sigh, my dear?" His Little Wife y (with whose beauty time is be- ginning to take liberties) : "Oh, nothing, serious, Jack; only I was thinking how much the mirrors have changed lately." " SAY, ma," remarked the small boy, " isn't it funny that everybody calls my little brother a bosucing baby ?" " Why do you think it is funny, William?" returned his in then " Because when I dropped him on the floor this morning he didn't bounce a bit." Dr7DET-r.iN (who had just put on a new suit of clothes in the shop): `, Wait a minute. I. 11- go over to the bank and get a cheque cashed." Thor (going out with him): " Very well—l'll follow suit." MEANING OF GENIUS. - One asked annther what the word ue,nius meant. The other said, "If you - had it in you, you would not ask the question; but, yoa have not, you will never know." YOUNG HOUSEIZEEeEIt. (to applicant for situation as cook) : "01 course I want some one who is experienced." Miss aFlanagra,n (triumphantly) : " Sure thin, ma'am, it's meaell that'll suit yez, for haven't I been with twinty families in the last rwo MOD t ?"</p>
        <p>A PARISHIONER inquired of his pastor the mean- ing of this line of Scripture : "He was clothed with curses as with a garment." "It signifies," replied the divine, " that the individual haA got a hat,it of sw arine:." Two soldiers ,ay under their blankets looking up at the star-. Jack : " What made you go into the army, Torn ?" Tom : " Vehl, I had no wife and I loved war, so I went. What made you go?' . Jack : " Well, I had a wife, and I loved peace, .Tom, so I went." WITH a great deal of trying and coaxing, Tam got Jock to join the volunteers. jock, being a kind of stupid fellow, was always out of step, and :could not comprehend any difficulty. One day, •while parading through the streets with the com- pany, his sister, perceiving he was out of step, -cried out, "Oh, mither, every ane o' the company is oot o' the step except oor Jock." "WILL you walk into the parlour?" said the -spider to the fly. " Well, hardly !" said the insect, as he winked the other eye; "Your .parlour has an entrance, but of exit it is shy, so stay outside in safely, and remain a little 'ily." AN enterprising phrenologist once wrote a note to the late Charles Dickens, asking permimion to make an examination of his cranium. Dickens</p>
        <p>repied : "Dear Sir,--At this time I require the use of my skull, but as soon as it shall be at leisured. will willingly pla:e it at your disposal." "WHAT is the meaning of p. '?" asked a would-be wit of a railway porter, as that functionary was posting up a time-bill at the station the other day. "Oh," was the ready reply, ":it means that we are punctual to . a moment." TAILOR: " Married or unmarried?" Customer: "Married." Tailor (to cutter) : "One pocket ,concealed in lining of vest." Customer: "Eli? What's that?" Tailor (explalning) : "To hide your chaage, you know, at night. I'm married :myself " A STORX is told of a lot of tinkers who had set ripen one man, and were ravnercifully beating him. Peesently there was a lull in the proceedings, and little deformed man, brandishing a very big 'stick, .elbowed his way through the crowd, shout- ing, "Cich, now, boys, for the love of mercy, let a poor little cripple have just cue stroke at him !" A SCOTCH clergyman, while talking to an old woman, a member of his congregation, asked her LOW many really good people she thought there were in the-parish. " Awed, minister," said the old dame, " there's mine I'm sure a' but jest oor twa set's, and I've sometimes had ma doots about you.,, ADVAYTAGES OF A THICK IfEAD.—A gentleman, few years since, havin4 brou4ht an action for an assault, his servant, was called as a witness to sup- port it, who, alter a few questions, observed " that he Was 'certain, if his master had not a very thick head, tlit blow which the defendant gave him • woad have cracked his skull." lti.-Nt;;lt, the Frei,ch actor, Lail once to call out to a fellow actor who was expected to-enter fr-in the right wing, " Ha, ha—there you are t" For some unaccountable reason, however, the actor cme on the stage from the left, side. P.,egnier, without being in the least disconcerte I, gave his call, and added, with a smile, "I. saw you in the look A (400 D SIGN. —The writer of a book of tr ivels puh:ished some years ago, relating the particulars of his being cast away, thus concludes : " After having walked e even hours without tracing the 'int of a human foot, to my great comfort and P' delight, I saw a MA.II hanging upon a gihbet. My pleasure at this cheering prospect was intxpres- sibie, for it convinced me I was in a civilised country." Bi74lNF.ss.—Trritat (1 Frenchman (to Ya,kee, who had taken hint for a waiter) " you have ,i.-r-rossly insulted me. There is my card. Aly seconds will vait upon you, sir r." Yankee : " Never mind your seconds, French:J. Von uan wait, upon Inc just as well. P4SS toe the WOreeSter eittl,.,; and he quick about it !•' A InGFILANn drover, before going to a fair, was acti,itotned to pray tha he might, twiLher cheat nor te cheated, but, if one of the two, rather to cheat them he cheated. FELLow (to friend pushing the twins in a perambulator): "II ilii. ill chappie ! How luw.); have you been a c;.acintun ?" M r, ied Fel:ow (hidiguautly) (1.0 cli Luau! What dyon mean Joviai Fellow : " Why, I see you are driving a carriage and oair."</p>
