      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>"CAPITAL" ()BAT.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" ()BAT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Chamberlain has just been made a subject of the 'graphologist," and as the professor of that most scientific of sciences gives his reports in "You and I"—the editor of which would be horrified if he were suspected of any political bias— we may take that the result of the operation has not been in any way affected by prejudice. The signature looks comparatively harmless, but it really hides a great deal. It shows, with unerring accuracy, that its proud possessor is active, energetic, ambitious, persevering, intellectually relined and has intensity of application. This is all very charming, but worse remains behind. The unkindest cut of all has to come. lam afraid that Mr. Chamberlain will have to call another big meet- ing to refute the statement and " pay back " his accuser. For " aristocratic assumption, self-con- sciousness and haughtiness are equally noticeable in conjunction with keen sensibilities; a calm and critical power of methodical observation, free decision, marvellous insight into character, and a disposition more inclined to the useful than to the ideal. Combativeness and resistance to assertion or fancied authority are also characteristics to be observed in this autograph, as are likewise love of order and calculation, lucidity of mind, tenacity of ideas, facility of composition, diplomacy and caution in expression, and an insatiable desire for knowledge, lame, and notoriety."</p>
        <p>"Aristocratic assumption, self-consciousness, and haughtiness." And this the one time democratic Jupiter, whose thunderbolts once caused so much consternation in the circles where blood is blue I I should like the graphologist to examine the signature of the right honourable gentleman previous to his "conversion."</p>
        <p>Sir William Harcourt has thought it well,in view of the rumours that have been afloat of late, to intimate to his friends that he has not arrived at any decision as to ,his personal position after the Budget Bill is passed. Much will depend on the way in which he is supported by the party, and there is reason to believe that he will place himself entirely, in their hands. He is prepared to do exactly what the majority of the Liberals in the House preier. I understand that as soon us the Finance Bill has been passed, a meeting of the party will be called to consider the situation.</p>
        <p>Sir Augustus Harris has soon tired of newspaper proprietorship. He acquired the "Sunday Times" two or three years ag'i and has now disposed ot it to a new proprietor. The "Sunday 'limes" is a journal which has passed through some curious vicissitudes of late years. It was owned and edited for a while by Col. Fitz-George, one of the sons of the Duke of Cambridge. One of its features during that period was its gossip about court and society. The rieXt proprietor was Yrs. Cornwell, a wealthy Australian lady, who was said to have been the pro- totype of one of Mr. ,Fergus Hume's heroines.</p>
        <p>Mr. Phil Robinscn was th e editor-in-chief during Mrs. Cornwell's time, and also during a portion oi Sir Augustus Harris's proprietorship. More recently the paper has been under the oirection of Mr. Arthur A'Beckett, the son of the London magistrate who was ,one of the chief contributors to "Punch," and who wrote the comic histories of England and of Rome. Mr. Arthur A'Becketz, is himself a " Punch" man, and his leading contribution to the "Sunday Times" has been a weekly political article of thP comic sort, purporting to come from "The Member for Wrottenborough."</p>
        <p>It has already been proved that the work "bet- terment," at which Lord Salisbury attempted to sneer as American slang, was used in old English, and now the very principle itself—and especially it connection with the Tower Bridge—is given the cachet of antiquity. Some interesting evidence is to be given, in the Lords' Committee, which is considered the general question. It has, I believe, been discovered that under the provisions of an Act passed quite early in the century, for the construction oi one of the greal bridges which now connect old and new Edinburgh a special rate was levied over a certain area which it was assumed, and rightly, would be enhanced in value by the improvement, and the City Chamber- lain of the northern capital is to be called to give evidence on the point.</p>
        <p>Mr. Julian Hawthorne having like Mr. Stevenson, a taste for tropical life, has recently purchased a large farm in the mountains of Jamaica, where he proposes to raise fruit and early vegetables for the northern maikets. lie has also rented a most beau- tiful residence, Mona, on a coffee estate near King- ston. A broad flight of black aid white marble steps lead to the front door, tall cocoanut palms shade it, and it has on one side a quaint old Italian garden lull of flowers, orange trees, and oleanders. A number of large outbuildings stand at the back, and in one of them is a swim ming bath thirty feet long. Another has been transformed into a study. Mr. Hawthorne himself papered it, and painted a most elaborate frieze of uaisies in a conventional pattern with a running inscription from the Persian around the top. The ceiling he painted in light yellow, and the heavy rafters in chocolate. The place is supposed to be haunted, but, to 3lr: Haw- thorne's disgust, the ghost refuses to appear. "He knows I would interview him, and use him as material."</p>
        <p>The present little imbroglio with France over our Congolese diplomacy, and the exhibition of the French love tor "worrying England," recalls to my mind a story I once heard of Ferdinand de Lesseps. He used to tell how a Frenchman came to his aid when he was struggling against Palmerston's oppo- sition to the Suez Canal. The man, who was a total stranger, walked into his office one day and drew out of his pocket a case stuffed with bank notes. "Monsieur," said Le," I beg the privilege of being allowed to subscribe toward the railway of the Island of Sweden." "But, monsieur," said Les- seps, "it is not a railroad, it's a canal; it's not an island, but an isthmus; it is not in Sweden, but at Suez." "I don't care what it is, or where it is," said the capitalist, "so long as it worries England. Put me down, I say."</p>
        <p>But Lesseps had little reason to make fun of this episode, if the picture his own son draws of him be true. Becoming incensed at some attack made on him in the British Parliament, and being inad- equately provided with means to express his rage, his mind suddenly reverted to his linen collar, which was of English make. Ile tore it from his . neck, dashed it upon the floor, and danced upon it—all for hatred of the island from which it came. All Paris is now engaged in a similar disarrangement of toilette—metaphorically speaking, of course.</p>
        <p>It is amusing to note the accidents by which the course of events in the House is sometimes deter- mined. The Government might easily have been placed in a serious difficulty on Wednesday if the Opposition had been able to rely on all their men. Quite a sufficient muster of Liberals were away at the Leeds Conference to turn the Government majority into a minority if the Conservative Whips had brought together all their forces. But Wednes- day happens to hay e been the second day of Ascot races, and the number of Conservatives who wanted to get away more than counterbalanced the num- ber of Liberals who wished to go to Leeds. As a result, therefore , the Government were able, on a question in which a considerable section of the Opposit ion were seriously concerned, to get their way, to show a large majority, and at the same time to put an end to the threatenedobstruction on the vote. The Irish Unionists, I hear, threaten to I raise some of the questions which were shut out on Wednesday on the Lord Lieutenant's vote, but it is certain that this will not give them the latitude they desire. The truth is that Wednesday's pro- ceedings have been singularly disheartening for the Unionists. Apart from the large Government majorities, certain of the speeches for their own side—notably the admissions of Mr. Horace Plunkett in regard to the improved state of Ireland—have given them anything but satisfaction.</p>
        <p>In Radical circles it is now taken for granted that the Cabinet have unexpectedly supported the Chancellor of the Exchequer, and that there will in consequence be no closure used in regard to the Finance Bill. This means, of course, that nothing else, except pt-rhaps the Evicted Tenants' Bill, will be passed. This is a big disappointment, but I fear that there is now no alternative. The Tories are making their arrangements on the understanding that the House will be up during the first week of A ugust.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>MOXON'S NURSERY BOON ;Unequalled for ESTROYING NITS, and PARASITES in Use HUMAN HAIR.) OF CHEMISTS and the PROPRIETOR, J. L. 31 X 0 N, 252, PAPE-ROAD. LIVERPOOL. 6c.. Awl Is. per box. Post Free 7i auct 1. reale mention, We paper.</p>
