      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURDAY, JUNE 23, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURDAY, JUNE 23, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Dimities, piques, ginghams, cottons, and linens of all kinds are the favourite dresses for this sum- mer's wearing. Very popular now are the narrow insertions and laces which are finished alike on each edge. These are both inserted and applied as passementerie would be. The popular silk blouse is almost universally trimmed with a lace insertion, either a real insertion with plain straight edges —that is, set in—or with one of the lace appliqués which has a fanciful edge on both sides. These narrow widths are seen upon ruffles of the silk, set a little way from the edge, one row being used upon each frill where there is more than one; on the deep rall.s sometimes two rows are employed. Cove colour is in great favour for linen dresses, and is certainly much less trying to the ordinary com- plexion than the popular holland. Another favourite tone is exactly that of a cochia china egg, the warmth of tint rendering it very pnui-kr. A dress which could be copied in less expaasi.re materials is in white more silk, spotted with stars in black, and trimmed with mauve velvet ribbons. A pretty fashion of combining a plain and fancy cloth is suggested by a thin black diagonal, arrangei with a full bodice and putled sleeves, having a vandyked yoke and waistbaril of blade watered silk. The skirt or overdtiss of the diagonal is caught very daintily on the left hip and fastened with a rosette; while the underskirt thus displayed is ~lao of blao moire.</p>
        <p>Among pretty 'hats is one of amber colour, with 4wo feathers arranged in quite unique fashion each aide of the crown, and a bow of amber moire and satin ribbon in the front. An extremely pretty though simple brown hat with straight brim of straw has a crown entirely of shaded roses tied round with a narrow brown velvet ribbon fastened by a tiny buckle. Equally charming in its way is a white rice straw with black bird and black and white satin rosettes. Burnt or black straw is trimmed with black or cream lace, and upstanding sprays of coloured roses, which are marvellous imitations of natural blossoms, while those fashion- able anachronisms, black roses, also appear with good contrasting effect. Another hat is of the toque order, which enjoys so much of our fashion- able favour just now. This is of black fancy straw, decorated with moire ribbon, quills, and flowers, though it can be copied in any other colour. It is a stylish little hat. In bonnets we are not permitted the choice between large and small, but then, who wants to wear a big bonnet when small ones are so chic and becoming? One of the very latest styles, the new Dutch shape, is made of black moire covered in applique guipure and trimmed with flowers both back and front, combined with sequin lace. A stylish hat, which shows a lace display, is made of pale pink straw, with two purple-winged birds poised an the brim in the front, whilst a third acts up at the back midst a spray of pink roses/.</p>
        <p>Dress for the children to-day is delightfully simple, pleasingly varied, and founded for the most part on common-sense lines, while the picturesque is allowed to obtain with appropriate becomingness. The young ones are now permitted by fashion to be dressed very like their mothers. Any girl from the age of eight upwards may be allowed to wear a linen shirt for bodiczi, and a simple skirt and double-breasted coat to complete the costume. The shirts for the children are all made in blouse style to overhang a belt, mad boast an elastic at the waist, while the collar are of the Eton shape. Silk shirts are warm. and innumerable smalls, foulards, and China sila:s are suitable for cold days. Tussahs make very warm and comfortable shirts. The fronts of these should be tucked, and the round collars which turn down from the neck are adorned with a feather-stitching in silk. Worn with red serge aud a red necktie this Tussah looks par- ticularly well. The children's neckties are all made of silk on the cross, with pointed ends, and</p>
        <p>the economical may be counselled to buy the silk and make these for themselves. A pretty little girl's frock which I have just seen is of maize. colour India muslin, ri3hly embroidered. It has a collar of smocking, wilh two deep frills of the embroidery, and the sleeves are full, with two frills at wrist.</p>
        <p>lovingly, JENNIE.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales, being asked on one occasion who was the cleverest woman he had ever met, promptly answered," My sister, the Empress Frederick."</p>
        <p>A woman has done for Rome what no male theatrical manager has ever been able to do. She has managed the opera season at the Argentine Theatre without a subsidy, and has been successful in every respect. The once beautiful and bewitching Countess of Beaconstein, transferred from Austria to Chicago, has taken pity on a local poet, Francis Zuri Stone, and will wed him. Stone achieved distinction and poverty sing- ing the praises of catarrh medicine. Mrs. Henry Ward Beecher says in regard to woman sufferage : "I am unalterably opposed to woman suff- rage. I cannot understand why women will not be satisfied with being women, without aspiring to man's estate."</p>
        <p>Miss Elizabeth L. Banks is preparing a book of her journalistic experiences in London, to be published by Cassell and Co. The book will contain portraits of the charming little "new journalist " in the guises of house and parlourmaid, flower girl, Chicago heiress, laundry girl, and various others. This reminds me, says a writer in "To-day," that I happened to be crossing the road at Baker-street Station one foggy and muddy afternoon, when I was addressed by a female crossing-sweep. I was about to put a penny in her hand, when, looking her rather closely in the face,'l saw that the crossing- sweep was no other than my friend the übiquitous Miss Banks; so I smiled, saved my penny, ar.d passed on.</p>
        <p>Education and Short Sight.</p>
        <p>That the requirements of work at English schools both for boys and girls are gradually rising, that more and more is required of them, is well known, and the lists of subjects cannot be read over by anyone familiar with children without feeling that either much of th 6, work must be scampcd (which is probably the case, or we should have more myopes amongst us) or that the scholars are worked at high pressure,and that the effects are likely to be injurious. At some boys' schools there are three half holidays in the week,which is an excellent ari angement, and probably, even in those who work hard, saves many from visual defects, especially as the games are such as for the most part require distant vision. Our arrangements for very young children are says the" Book of Health "perhaps the worst. Half an hour, or three quarters of an hour is as long as a child of six or seven can devote his mind to any object, and he should than be allowed a rest ; and it would be well if in English schools more use were made of the black-board and chalk, as is customary in Kindergarten establishments. Since, on the one hand, the employ- ment of the eyes on minute objects for any length of time is injurious, and, on the other, education which renders such employment of the eyes compulsory must be pursued, it follows that the amount of work that any cl.ild is called upon tl do must be of the nature of a compromise between the two ; and perhaps, the best compromise that can be made is that work should not be carried on unintermittingly, but that it should alternate with periods of rest—that mental exertion should be relieved by bodily exercise.</p>
        <p>Hint 4 for the Sick Room. 'The following useful information is taken from that excellent little magazine, "The Housewife" Only a small quantity of either food or drink should be given to a sick peraon at a time. A clean newspaper often renewed makes a better table- cover for a sick room than a cloth.</p>
        <p>Common baking soda is the best of remedies in cases of scalds and burns. It may be used on the surface of the burned place either dry or moistened. To prepare an egg for an invalid, beat until very light; add seasoning to taste, and then steam till thoroughly warm through. The most delicate stomach will be able to digest it. The French method of administering castor oil is to pour the oil into a pan over a moderate fire, break an egg into it and stir it well ; when it is done, flavour with a little salt or sugar or currant jelly. A medical journal states that a few handfuls of com- mon salt thrown daily into closets, and an occasional handful into wash basins, goes far toward counteracting the noxious effects of the omnipres2nt sewer gas. A little daily change in the arrangement of furniture, a little planning for such pleasant surprises as an invalid can bear in the way of food, company, and amusement, will often be more effectual in hastening the convalesc- ence of a patient than a score of tonics. An agreeable dish for an invalid's tea can be prepared by toasting dry cruAts of bread in a moderate oven until they are brown. When cold pound them in a mortar until reduced to a coarse meal, then sift it through a flour sieve into a dish. Serve with milk or cream and sugar, if desired. How to Cure Hiccough.—See how close together you can hold the tips of your forefingers without touching. Now keep your elbows out free from your side. You can get your fingers closer than that. They are touching now. There—now hold them so. Steady ! Then ask : 6. Now, why don't you hiccough ?" Hall's "Journal of Health" says that half a teaspoon- ful of common table salt dissolved in a little cold water and drank will instantly relieve heartburn or dyspepsia. If taken every morning before breakfast, increasing the quantity gradually to a teaspoonful of salt and a tum- bler of water, it will in a few days cure any ordinary case of dyspepsia, if at the same time due attention is paid to diet.</p>
        <p>THE DOUBLE LIFE;</p>
        <p>OR, THE HAMPTON MYSTERY.</p>
