      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MR. A. M. MOND AND MISS VIOLET GOETZE.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MR. A. M. MOND AND MISS VIOLET GOETZE.</p>
        <p>At the Church of St. Mark's, Hamilton-terrace, Lon- don, N.W., on Saturday afternoon, by special licence, and with full choral service, the Rev. Canon Duckworth, D.D.,of Westminster, chaplain to the Queen, officiating, the marriage took place of Mr. Alfred Mond Mond, of the Temple-gardens, son of Mr. Ludwig Mond. F.R.S., of The Poplars, Avenue-road, Regent's Park, N.W., and Winningtun Hall, Cheshire, and Miss Violet Goetze, daughter of the late Mr. James Henry Goetze, of Hamil- ton Lodge, Upper Hamilton-terrace, London, N.W. A brother of the bride (Mr. Leopold Goetze) gave her away. The bridegroom was attended by Mr. Roscoe Brunner as best man. The bride wore a wedding dress of the richest pearl white Lyons satin, made perfectly plain, en princesse, trimmed only with old Brussels point lace, and full court train—all the gift of her mother. Her fine veil enclosed a wreath of orange blofsoms. Her ornaments were a diadem of diamonds, with a diamond pear-shaped pearl, the gift of Mr. and Mrs. Mond ; and a diamond and pearl necklace, the gilt of the bride- groom. There were five bridesmaids—Miss Angela Goetze (sister of the bride), Miss Constance Schneich and Miss Teresa Mathias (cousins of the bridegroom), Miss Brunner, and Miss Hale, who were charmingly at- tired. The bridesgroom'4 presents to them were brooches of diamends and amethyst in form of spray of violets and bouquets of white flowers and violets in foliage. Two pages, the son and daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Care- meyes, acted as pa,c,,e4rainbearers, both attired in white satin costumes of the time of Charles I. The reception given by Mrs. Goelor at her town residence, Hamilton Lodge, after the ceremony was very largely attended, among those present being Mr. and Mrs. Ludwig Mond, Mr. Brunner, M.P.. and Mrs. Brunner. the Rev. Canon Duckworth, D.D.. Professor and Mrs. Dewar, Mr. Woot- ton Isaacson, M.P., Mrs.Wootton Isaacson, Dr. ant Mrs. Mackenzie, Mr. T. P. O'Connor, M.P., and Mrs. T. P. O'Connor. Afterwards Mr. and Mrs. Alfred M. Mond left for Little Marlow, en route for Deal, for the honey- moon. The presents, over 300, were handsome and costly.</p>
      </div>
