      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>SCHOOL OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>HA)2DWRITIN(I,—i;i Cia-sr-s With distinction: Bate, H. P. and Class: Bailey, S.; Baines, G. W. ; Barton' J. ; Fair- Clough, J.; Greene, H.; Payne, S. 3rd class :Clarke, G.; liorobin, J. El.; Taylor, F. SHORTHAND, speed 2nd class: Dakin, W.; Walker, E. V. 3rd class : Kirk, E. W.; Roberts, H. G. Not classed: Roughsedge, Lilian. Elementary passed: Crank, W. T.; -EYe, I. W.; Haviour, H. ;Horobin, C.; Roberts, H. A. ; Sumner, H.; Williams, G. 800K-BEEPING, 2nd class: Bate, J. S. Knowles, H. S.; aackey, G. E.; Owen, T. H.•, Pharaoh, J. 3rd class • Stringer, J. H. Not classed: Baldry, S. 111111 ARITHMETIC (ADVANCED), Ist class: Bate, J. S. 2nd class : Bailey, S. 3rd class: Allen, H. W. Bate, H. P.; °akin W.; Plevin, T. Commercial:grade not classed : Rate, T. D. WExousa (CommEncrAL), 3rd class: Bate, J. S. ; Jean,</p>
        <p>FRENCH (ELEMENTARY), 2nd class : Batc?,H. B.; Fowler, Nellie; Holford, G. B.; Kidd, Evangeline M. ; Taylor. Pt G. ; Wray, G. W. 3rd class : Foster, Gertrude; porse, Catherine M.; Humphreys, Sarah E.; Shaw,Ellen; Ilhompson, Edith E. Advanced Literary 2nd class: Pickton, A. 3rd class: Owen, T. H.; Scotsin, G. A. SPANISH (ADvaNcED), 3rd class: Gerrard, E. J. (Ele- Mentary) 2nd class: Simcock. F. 3rd class : Crank, J. Not classed : Jones, E. W. ; Pilkington, W. ,_GERMAN (ADVANCED LITERARY), 2nd class: Harring- ton, J. C. 3rd class: Lyon, C.; Sutton, R. (Elemen- 'arY) 2nd class: Cuppage. Agnes. TaEorur OF MUSIC (ELEMENTARY), ISt class: Broad- hurst, J. K. ; Cooper, Kate; Clay, Leah ; Fowler, Nellie; Ireland, W. H.; Jones, H. C.; Pickava,nce, Gertrude; Shaw, Ellen. 2nd class: Barlow, Edith A. ; Broadhurst, Emily ; Gorse, Catharine M.; Kidd, Pauline R. 3rd Class: Humphreys, Sarah E.; Pickavance, Eva. DRESSMAKING, Ist class: Berry, Emma V.; Carr, Edith ; Gartland, Daisy; Hayes, Annie; Parsons, Eva; Richardson, Elizabeth. 2nd class: Armstrong, Alice M. ; Hare, Margaret A. ; Hill, Francis; Mather, Mary ; Rigby, I.larY ; Stone, Maggie G. ; Taylor, Alice W.; Whittaker, Sarah. 3rd class : Carey, Asnie ; Critchley, Eva; Pod- Tore, Elizabeth. Whitelegg, Agnes M. Not classed : "ones, Margaret J.</p>
        <p>WA RRINGTON INFIRMARY. The irnportant meeting Gf the Infirmary Com- mittee the other week discloses a state of finances of that institution which is far from satisfactory to itS nsanag at the e and which demands speedy remedy hands of the public. A few years ago the pressing necessity for enlargement was commented on in these columns. It was shown that the accommodation at the Infirmary was totally inadequate to the require- Inents of a town of the size and importance of Warring- ton. Subsequently by public subscription a fund was raised for the enlargement and equipment of the hospital The rsquisite enlargements and fresh buildings (the new dispensary and out-patient departments) have re- cently been completed, and the hospital is now quite abreast with the times as to amount of accommodation and efficiency of equipment. It has, in fact, been pro- nounced by high authority to be a model of what a hospital should be. This result has only been attained "sin spite of the strictest economy and closest supervision by the committee—by the expenditure 'lf a sum greatly in excess of that originally contemplated. To put it plainly, the committee are met face to face with a deficit of £1,286 153. 101 This unavoidable liability has been rendered necessary in a great degree to compensate for past insufficiency. The hospital had fallen behind the large bsth in accommodation and equipment, and a rge outlay was necessary to restore its efficiency and Place it on a basis of thorough usefulness to our town. 'sbat money could not be spent in a better cause goes ?sithout saying, and the recital of a few statistics will illustrate to our readers the worthy return they have had for the support hitherto accorded to so useful an I.ostitutioo. The ne v Infirmary was opened in 1877,and In the first year of its existence administered to the wants of 87 in-patients, 2,940 out and home patients, and 449 accidents. Contrast those fis'ures with those of last Year, 1893, when there were 343 in-patients, 3,792 out and home patients, and 1,318 accidents. When we iadd that the total aggregate of patients treated at the °°snital since its opening reaches the enormous number I)3` 82,542, the public must recognize that the money they w__a•eve subscribe.d for the r?lief of human suffering as been th 11 alad worthily directed. It must be apparent also that cr! incrsase in accommodation for patients and the in- number thereof has necessitated a corresponding crease in the executive, which has ben more than .I_oubled to meet the augmented work. That t';.e main- hen_ance of this extra staff and larger number of patients 84eeessitated not only a large immediate outlay, but 011 calls imperatively for a larger e,aclowinent and actual 4colne , 3 a self-evident proposition. But what have the ' Intrhttee to face? Either money to meet the deficit _must be immediately raised, or the capital of the Infir- mary must tie drawn upon. This latter alternative is ue whichevery well-wisher of the hospital would shrink ~M in alarm. It would seriously cripple the hospital ud diminish the income, which would only necessitate freshti s unwholesome resource will not be needed. What n aFrington has done Warrington coo do. Already a s• "ght example has been set in this direc- fon as Charl will be seen from the letters of Mr. ap ton Parr and Mrs. Beam ant, which are bouPieltlided. We' have a wealthy aristocracy in our neigh- ood, and can rely on their support. To those of er char' means we would say " Every little helps," and fu_uu could find n) worthier channel than in providing We to relieve the necessities of the sick and needy.. rnittfeel confident that the appeal of the Infirmary Com- p, ee for funds to complete their noble work will be ,IPtIY and amply met. In no town in the kingdom hyr, the vvorkinginan (under their present admirable o;„anr';fizati°lll) contribute so large as ours do to our 4,,.„a1, thus abundantly testifying to its good work hn'ngst them. That their effort i ought, can and 'will -I"ornpleted by their richer brethren admits of no doubt ~, • nateyer.</p>
        <p>[copy.] Orford Hall, Warrington. June 3rd, 1894. t Dear Mr. Dakin,-1 am so sorry that the Infirmary is heetcling mtieli help that I enclose a cheque for £l9O to _eiP a little. If only a few friends to the institution 7ould give generously, the threatened bazaar would not oe needed. After a while, if needed, I will give another contribution. With sincere wishes for the welfare of the Infirmary, ad very kind regards to Mrs. Dakin and yourself, I am, Sincerely youni, L. BEAMONT. [COPY.] (lrappenhall Heyes, Warrington, May 27th, 1894. _Dear Sir,—l am sorry I cannot name a day to preside t:l4 a Meeting- t tie Infirmary. lam leaving home again t,:daY for a fortnight or three weeks. I much regret uat it should he necessary to trench upon our invested Pital to pay our debts. I s.hould have much pleasure giving £lOO towards the payment of the debt if the tnittee could see their way to raising the remainder. Yours faithfully, J. CHARLTON PARR. J. L. Tunstall, Esq.</p>
