      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB'S ATHLETIC FESTIVAL</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CYCLING CLUB'S ATHLETIC FESTIVAL</p>
        <p>Whatever misgivings Warrington cyclists may have had of the very unseasonable weather which has been experienced of late, they may congratulate themselves upon having chosen a clay for their annual athletic festival which was in every way suitable for the occasion.. Most acceptable sunshine prevailed on Saturday, in striking contrast to the March-like weather of the previous day. There would be about 2,000 spectators on the Rugby Football Club's ground, Wilderspool Causeway, when the starter fired off his first shot for the afternoon, in the two miles bicycle handicap, limited to members of the Warrington Cycling Club, which was given the foremost place in the pro- , gramme. The track was in very good condition, con- I sidering the rains which had fallen during the week. The officials responsible for the arrangements were the following, to whom much credit is due for the successful afternoon's sport, which was carried through without the slightest hitch :—Judges: Messrs. J. E. Warren, W. Lomas and R. Andrews; starter. Mr. E. Kane, Salford Harriers; handicapper, Mr. W. Platt, (N.C.A.A.) ; referee, Mr. John Evans; hon. sec., Mr. F. Garnett; assistants, Messrs. D. Davies and J. B. Webster; trea- surer. Mr. W. Mounfield ; lap scorers, Messrs. W H. Bellian and E. Pickavance, jun. ; committee: Messrs. W. Brown, A. Booth, T. Davies, S. Wright, G. W. Whit- ham, J. Podmore, T. Pemberton, P. Fair, J. Buxton, F. Molyneux, C. H. Wood, H. Leigh, J. Whitham and J. Critchley. During brief intervals selections of music were played by the Borough Brass Band. As has already been stated the club bicycle handicap was the first event. For this there were 24 entries, and it was run off in three heats. The handicap was per- haps noticeable for the non-success of the " favourites." J. Buxton was beaten in the first heat, as were also J. Davies, and A. T. Dainteth in the third heat. The latter raced well, but was rather too heavily handicapped, Newcombe with 210 yards start of him came in first,and J. Whitham with 280 yards was second in the heat. The six cyclists in the final was T. Hearns, T. Astle, Albert Foster, W. E. Charnock, B. Newcombe and J. Wnitham. &amp;stle was unfortunate in having a " spill " early in the race. Newcombe was an easy first, followed by Reams and Charnock. The prizes were Ist, gold watch, pre- , sented by the president kMr. T. Morris) ; 2nd, case of carvers; and 3rd, silver cup, presented by Mt. Percy Davies. There were 52 entries in the 100 yards flat race handi- cap, and some evenly contested heats were witnessed. Only three Warrington men won in the eleven heats,and in the succeeding trial heats these three were vanquished Their names were Bickford, Horan, and Cunningham. The final race was therefore confined to three outsiders. I W. Rawsthorne, of Liverpool led from the first, but the second and third were very evenly matched. W.Nixon, of the Ditton F.C., however, got second by a few inches, and F. R. Townsend, Sunlight F.C., was third. The first prize was an electro tea service, value £5 ; 2nd, large cruet stand (six bottles), value £2 ; and 3rd, breakfast cruet, value £l. _ . .</p>
        <p>Several well-known cyclists in the district had entered the one mile open bicycle handicap. Only one Warring- ton cyclist pulled through his heat, viz., J. Buxton. The race was noticeable for some excellent riding, and some close finishes. The six competitors in the final were— J. J. Galway, Chester ; J. Buxton, Warrington ; W. Sherratt, Widnes ; J. Morton, Lancaster ; W. Turner, Cheetham ; E. J.Holmes, Burslem ; E.Jackson,Bowdon ; and J. Gibson, Standish. This latter cyclist led the way in the first few laps,but was overtaken by Jackson,who led for a short distince. Hie success was but brtef, for Gibson shot forward.and carried off the first prize. Gal- way and Morton were second and third respectively. Prizes—First, fitted Gladstone bag„ value ; second, electro-plated kettle, value £2 ; third, barometer, value £l. In the 220 yards flat race handicap there were 46 en- tries. T. Horan, Warrington, was first in the first heat, and T. Davies, Altrinchatn, and J. Robinson, Warring- ton, were a dead heat for second place. The other three heats were won by T. West 111. Hollinfare ;J. Houghton, Warrington ;H. Ward, Altrincham ; P. E. Hill, Sea- combe ; J. C. Leathwood, Runcorn F.C., and J. Massey, Warrington. The final was a good race, Horan securing the first prize. Davies and Leathwood were at his heels for second and third places. The following were the prizes: Ist. marble clock, value £3; 2nd, barometer, value £2 ; 3rd, small Gladstone bag value £l. The most interesting item in the afternoon's pro- gramme was the two miles bicycle handicap (open). Again were Warrington cyclists unsuccessful in the several heats, not one being able to enter the final. Some very clever riding was witnessed. H. Brown of Leigh, who was scratch man in the first heat, easily passed his opponents, and got in first. G. J. Burrows of Chester, was second. F. C. del Strother, of the Anfleld 8.C., and J. Gibson, Standish, were the winners of the second heat, and J. J. Galway, Chester, and W. Jackson, Widnes, won the third heat. The final was a most ex- citing race. Brown was again scratch man, but in a few laps he caught up the other competitors, and looked to be a likely winner. But Burrows, of Chester, by a grand spurt led the way, with Jackson, of Widnes, fol- lowing very closely. Brown was a good third. The first prize was a gold albert, value £4; 2nd prize, silver Prescot watch, value £2, and 3rd prize, gent's silver mounted umbrella, value £l. About thirty competitors ran in the one mile flat race, and one of the most conspicuous features of the race was the very marked falling off of runners as the race progressed. This was to some extent due to the weather which was rather too warm :for a one mile foot race. C.H. Gable of Altrincham,who started "scratch," although unsuccessful, ran well. E. H. Pultord of Sef- ton was first, A. Looney, Douglas, second, and F. Fletcher, Heywood, third. The prizes were Ist, silver keyless watch value £4, 2nd, regulation clock, value £2, 3rd, dressing case, value £l. Mrs. T. Morris distributed the prizes to the successful competitors at the close of the proceedings. The silver cup won by Charnock in the members' race was with- held for a few weeks, until he has completed the neces- sary number of runs with the club. Mr. Davies,captain of the Cycling club, thanked Mr. Morris and Mr. P. Davies for the prizes they had given for the Cycling club race,and also thanked Mrs. Morris for her presence. Mr. P. Davies and Mr. T.Morris responded, the latter gentle- man moving a vote of thanks to the secretary, Mr. Garnett.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEILTiI OF WARRINGTON, Week ending 2na June, 1894. During the week, which was the 22nd of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warrington 4l 17 Latchford 4 3</p>
        <p>Total 45 20 The excess of births over deaths was 25. The death- rate from all causes was equal to 16.9 per 1,000, against 18 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 23.5 the corresponding week of last year. The death- rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 4.6 per 1,000. against 2.2 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 9.4 the corresponding week of 1893. The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-60 60 up. 5 4 1 4 3 17 1 1 1 3</p>
        <p>The deaths included— 3 from scarlet fever, 2 from whooping ccugh, 4 from phthisis, 5 from lung disease, 3 from brain disease, 1 from scalds, 2 from debility. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever 7 cases. Patients in hospital on 27th May, 27. METEOROLOGY. —The mean temperature was 48-1 Fah., and therefore 4-7 deg. higher than the preceding week The maximum temperature 613 F., was recorded on the 2nd inst., and the minimum temperature 34 F., on the Ist inst. The rainfall measured •47 inch. The general direction was S.W., the reading of the Anemometer denoting 985 miles.</p>
        <p>JOIIN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
