      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BATCH OF CASES.</head>
        <p>A BATCH OF CASES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court, on Friday morning, Inspector Luff, 8.5.P.C.A., brought before the Bench a number of defendants charged with cruelty to animals.—Martin Lyons, greengrocer, Bank-street, was charged with cruelty to a horse by working it in an unfit state on the Ist of June. Mr. Luff stated that he saw the defendant, with a pony draw- ing a cart containing onions. The animal was very old, in an emaciated condition, and utterly unfit for work. P.C. Amson gave corroborative evidence. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed. Alfred Lawless, a youth, was charged with working a pony in an unfit state, and Samuel Lawless, fish dearer, Butterrnarket-street was charged with causing the animal to be so worked. Defendants pleaded guilty. The Inspector stated that the animal was lame on the off fore leg' and was suffering from contracted tendons and wasted muscles. The animal was completely worn out. Sergeant raylor corroborated. The defendant (Mr. Lawless) stated that he had done away with the pony. He was fined ss. and costs, and the ch arge against Alfred Lawless was dismissed. James Green, Lower Walton, was also charged with working a horse whilst in an unfit state, on the 6th inst. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, appeared on behalf of the defendant. Inspector Luff stated that he was at Market Gate at 6.40 p.m. on the date named, when he saw a ' youth in charge of a milk cart, to which a bay mare was attached. The horse stopped in Bridge- ' street and the driver was unable to make it go again without using the whip. The animal soon afterwards stopped again and witness went and examined it. He found that both knees were badly broken, and on one knee there was a wound which had been filled up with some black greasy substance. Witness questioned the lad in charge, and he admitted that the aaimal had been lame about two weeks. The driver then took hold of the reins and said she was all right, and drove away. Constable Jones spoke as to the lameness of the animal. Mr. Browne contended that there was no cruelty in the matter. He would call Mr.Hewitt,veterin- ary surgeon, who saw the animal immediately after the officer. The pony was in excellent condition, and was not lame. A slight scar was on the knee, which was caused by a lad throwing a hoop at Walton, three weeks previously, which legged the horse I down, but the horse was not suffering any pain. Mr. Hewitt said he examined the pony ont he date named. The knees were an eyesore, but the horse was not suffering any pain. 'I he knees had been troken about four years ago. There was no wound, and the horse had been working since the day the officer saw it. Mr. Robert Green, farmer, Lower Walton, said the animal could go a mile in four minutes, and said it had broken knees when he bought it. Mr. Edwin Toole, cabman, and Mr. Hatton, landlord of the Seven Stars Inn, Bridge-st., also gave evidence for tha defence. The magistrates dismissed the case. Wm. Whitfield was charged with having caused to be cruelly illtreated, a horse, by causing it to be worked on the 7th June. P.C. Jones gave evidence as to seeing the animal attached to a milk cart on the date named in Dial-st. The horse was very lame. Inspector Luff said he saw the horse in a field. It was suffering from ring- bone, and there was a considerable amount of inflam- mation, It was unfit for work. A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CRIPPLE'S EXTRA.ORDINARY</head>
        <p>A CRIPPLE'S EXTRA.ORDINARY</p>
        <p>RESTORATION.</p>
        <p>A remarkable occurrence is reported, after a personal investigation, by the "Derbyshire Advertiser." Mr. Joseph Blackwell, of Pump-lane, Winster, who had been almost crippled in the legs and helpless in the hands, was, at the age of 75, actually enabled to resume his occupation, beside walking to and from his work over a stiff hill.</p>
        <p>The reporter's narrative is very interesting. 111 r. Blackwell, who was found actively at work, farming,had been a groat sufferer from rheumatism for seven or eight years. Both legs had been afflicted, and the pain in his hands was very acute. The arms, shoulders, and neck, had also been affected. His legs and feet were like logs ef wood. Be obtained no rest at night, the pain was so igtense. He had felt that he should never again be able to walk as he had been accustomed, nor to work as hitherto. But he was overjoyed to say that now all rheumatism and pain had left him, that he was able to go about his work with case, and that he could get proper rest . _ _ .</p>
        <p>" How was this change brought about?" asked the interviewer,</p>
        <p>The answer was very enthusiastic. His daughter had read in a newspaper of the extraordinary cures effected by a remedy unlike any ether medicine, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and Mr. Blackwell determined that he would try these pills. He had tried many remedies, and had been in a hospital, but the relief he received seemeJ to be only of a temporary kind. Before he had taken half a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, how- ever, he found permanent relief, and as he kept on tak- ing the remedy, the pain left him altogether, and had not since returned.</p>
        <p>"Then do you feel freedom in the use of your limbs?' questioned the reporter. " I could scarcely walk," was the reply of the former cripple, "but now I climb the hills about here easily." Dr. Williams Pink P ills are an unfailing cure for rheu- matism, sciatica, neuralgia, partial paralysis, locomotor ataxy, St. Vitus dance, nervous headache, prostration, &amp;c. They give the glow of health, are a specific for troubles of the female sex, and in men effect a radical cure in cases arisihg from mental worry, overwork, or excesses of any kind. Th ey are sold by all chemist q; or may be had direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Corn pany, 46, Holborn Via duct, London, at 2s. 91 a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are never sold loose, and any dealer who offers substitutes is try- ing to defraud; the wmoden box must be in a pink wrap- per, bearing the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. Not a purgative.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>POOR LANDLORDS. AN " APPALLING " PROSPECT.</head>
        <p>THE POOR LANDLORDS. AN " APPALLING " PROSPECT.</p>
        <p>According to a report in the "Globe," Lady Gwendolen Cecil has published a leaflet on the Death Duties in which working men are expected to take a deep inter- est. Lady Gwendolen "shows" that the question is one which affects the working classes very deeply indeed. "If this tax," says the fair pamphleteer. " is passed by the House of Commons, whenever a landlord dies the new landlord will have to pay such an enormous sum to the Government that in the great majority of cases he will be forced to shut up his house and leave his home for several years ; many rJuch men will never be able to live in their places again." "Will not that mean some- thing," asks Lady Gwendolen Cecil " to working men who live in the country ?" The prospect is appalling, says the "Manchester Guardian," and will no doubt strike terror to the heart of the rural labourer as he draws his magnificent weekly wage. Lady Gwendolen does not invite the labouring classes and the shopkeepers and merchants to come forward with an extra twopence in the pound on the income tax in order tb relieve people who come into great estates from enforced exile for "several years." Meanwhile it may be observed that the Duke of Portland, who already owns a considerable slice of this island, has purchased " for a little under a quarter of a million " an estate in Ayrshire. If the owner of Welbeck should proceed to accumulate landed property in this reckless fashion, the next Duke will, if Lady Gwendolen Cecii i right,he obliged to live in some nook hf the Isle of Man for many years. I hear, writes a London correspondent, that the leaflet has produced an effect quite the opposite to that which was intended by the authoress. The leaflet has been largely circulated by the Primrose League ; but its only result has been to convert nearly all who have read it into staunch sup- porters of the Budget. The Tory organisations in the constituencies are advising the withdrawal of the leaflet from circulation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>COAL TRADE. _-- AN ABORTIVE MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE COAL TRADE. _-- AN ABORTIVE MEETING.</p>
        <p>As had been anticipated, the representatives of the miners did not, attend the meeting of the Conciliation Board at the St. Martin's Town Hall, on Tuesday. Regret at their absence was expressed by the Chairman (Mr. Clifford Smith) and Mr. Hewitt and the opinion was expressed that the representatives of the men were under a misapprehension as to the meaning of the rule under which the meeting had been called. Eventually it was decided to adjourn the meeting to the 3rd of July.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
