      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>WARRINGTON MILLOWNERS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MILLOWNERS</p>
        <p>AND THE Km' CANAL.</p>
        <p>THE SUPPLY OF WATER, TO THE RIVER.</p>
        <p>TERMS OF SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>This action has been settled, subject to the approval of the Warrington Corporation, who have a clause in the Ship Canal Act, 1885, regulating the quantity of water to be used for lockage at the Latchford locks. Previous to the construction of the canal all the water of the Mersey passed down through Warrington, and a large proportion of the ordinary flow was used by the water- wheels at the large flour mills of Messrs. Fairclough. If the agreement is carried out the question of compensa- tion for the abstraction of this water at Latchford will be finally determined. The works named in the agree- ment are sluices. They were proposed to be carried out some time back, and are included in the estigiate for completion of works. Messrs. Faircleugh, during the passage of the Ship Canal Bill through Parliament, obtained a clause giving them compensation for any interference with their rights of water or navigation. The terms of arrangement are as follows : During such times as the navigation of the river Mer- sey between the Mersey Mills and Runcorn is in such a condition that Messrs. Fairclough's vessels cannot navi- gate it as heretofore' Messrs. Fairclough are to have the right of navigation tor their vessels free of toll between Weston Mersey lock and their Mersey and Bank Quay Mills, if the want ot navigable depth in the river Mersey is not due to any act or default on the part of the Canal Company. The Canal Company are to construct and maintain such works at Butchersfield. and if necessary between that point and Warrington, as will enable them to comply with sub-section 12 of section 88, of the Ship Canal Act 1885, with a modification that if water in excess of 200 million cubic feet per day is flowing past Howley Quay the Canal Company are to be at liberty to allow the excess to pass down the Canal past Latch- ford locks. If any difference arises as to the works to be executed by the Canal Company, or as to their main- tenance, it is to be referred to Mr. James Mansergh, or, him failing, an arbitrator to be appointed by the Board of Trade. The works are to be completed by the 30th April next. The Canal Company upon approval of the agreement by the Warrington Corporation are to pay Messrs. Fairclough the sum of £lo,ooo,which sum shall, together with the rights mentioned in clause 1 hereof,be accepted by Messrs Fairclough in full satisfaction of the following matters, that is to say- 1. Compensation and damages in respect of all injury to Messrs. Fairclough in respect of navigation to the Mersey Mills and Bank Quay Mills, either past or future. 2. Compensation and damages in respect of all loss of water power at the Mersey Mills up to the comple- tion of the works mentioned in clause 2 hereof, and also any loss of water power at the Mersey Mills which may be caused by reason or in consequence' of the exe- cution or due working by the Canal Company of the works authorized by the Ship Canal Act, 1835, or these presents. But nothing herein contained shall prejudice or affect Messrs. Fairclough's right of action or right to compen- sation if the Canal Company neglect or fail to perform the stipulations of this agreement or the provisions of the said Act (except as modified hereby). The 'Canal Company are not to alter the present level of Howley Weir or of the sluices at Latchford locks. The Canal Company are to pay the costs as between solicitor and client, such costs to be taxed in case of difference. The action to stand over until 10th July next, to enable the Canal Company to obtain the assent of the Warrington Corporation to this argeement. If such consent shall not be obtained on or before • that date the action to be restored at the instance of either party, and these terms shall be void.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>_.•-, COWARDLY ASSAULT UPON A</head>
        <p>_.•-, COWARDLY ASSAULT UPON A</p>
        <p>which he took out of his pocket. Witness knocked it out of his hand with his stick.</p>
        <p>All the defendants denied having assaulted the con- stable saying that they went straight home peacefully from the Red Lion. Joseph Grady denied having threa- tened to stab Hassall. He did not have a knife in his possession that night. He added that he was followed by two men, whom he did not know, who knocked him down several times and kicked him.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said that he had been instructed by the Chief Constable to prosecute in this case. Irishmen came over to England during the harvesting season ,and many of them disturbed their usually quiet villages and attacked constables who had been left alone to preserve order. He asked the magistrates to make an example of these men in order that they might know that they must conduct themselves in a peaceful manner. The police were extremely obliged for the timely and valu- able assistance rendered by the three witnesses, and he (Mr. Browne) had been asked to thank them on behalf of the constabulary. Only last year a constable was nearly killed through being assaulted in a similar man- ner at Knutsford.</p>
        <p>The magistrates sentenced the prisoners to one month's imprisonment at Knutsford , with hard labour, without the option of paying tines.</p>
        <p>_....-o_ LTMM.</p>
        <p>EXCURSION TO SorTHrorrr.—An excursion in connec- tion with St. Margaret's Church. Altrincham, was run to Southport on Saturday and a large party in Lymm took advantage of the reduced prices to pay a visit to the favourite watering place. Southport was reached in good time, and the day being delightfully fine, the visit was much enjoyed. The excursionists were enabled to visit the various places of amusement at reduces prices on production of their railway tickets, and this was a feature that was much appreciated. Home wa reached about 10.50 p.m. DRUNKENNESS.— Michael Cunningham an Irish labourer of Haydock, was charged at the Lymm Police Court, on Monday, before Dr. Brabazon with having been drunk on Rush-Green en the 17th June.—P.C. Breeze proved the case, and the defendant was fined 2s. ed., or in default seven days' imprisonment. —The money was paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>CONSTABLE BY IRISH HA RVESTMEN.</head>
        <p>CONSTABLE BY IRISH HA RVESTMEN.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday before Messrs. H. Reynolds, S. Taylor, and J. Handley, eight Irish harvestmen named Patrick Bones, Thos. Regan, Patrick Reed, Patrick Digna, John Digna, Roger Grady, Joseph Grady. and Thomas Bones, were charged with being drunk and disorderly at Hal- ton on the night of the 16th inst., and with assaulting P.C. Juett whilst in the execution of his duty.—Mr. Arthur Browne,solicitor,Warrington.appeared on behalf of the police. All the defendants admitted being drunk, but denied the assault. Mr. Browne in opening the case pointed out that the Bench had large discretionary powers given them in dealing with cases such as this, and expressed a hone that if the prisoners were found guilty they would be sentenced to a sufficiently ion.' term of imprisonment to meet the justice of the case. 'The facts were,that on the previous Saturday night P.C. Juett heard the landlord of the Red Lion turning the prisoners out of the house, and went towards the premises with the object of ren- dering assistance. When they were off the premises the constable told them to go home, and folloy.ed behind, being anxious to make the village quiet. Instead of being peaceful, and going home properly, they began to quarrel amongst themselves, and on reaching Halton Cemetery they turned up4n the constable,who was alone, knocked him down, kicked him, and split his head open. Two witnesses did a plucky thing. Hearing the officer's cry for help, they came upon the scene,and after scme time succeeded in driving the men away with stones. After the assault Joseph Grady threatened to stab a young man named William Hassall with a knife,Grady probably being aware that Hassall was on his way to Preston Brook for police assistance. The latter, however, when he saw Grady raise the knife knocked it out of his hand with a stick. Continuing. Mr. Browne said that some of the prisoners were perhaps under the influence of drink but that was no excuse whatever as all of them knew what they were doing. They knew that whatever one of their party did all of them were responsible for and that there was no distinction. The constable was at present under the treatment of Dr. Ormsby. His arms were numb, his head was very badly cut and bruised, and also one of his thighs the result of the kick- ing. He had used his staff on the night in question ; indeed he would have been foolish if he had not done so. But whatever he did was only in self defence and the prisoners deserved what they got. All the prisoners were afterwards apprehended at different farm houses in the locality and had all been identified. P.C. Juett after detailing the circumstances which led up to the assault said that when they arrived at the cemetery two or three of them jumped upon his back, threw him to the ground, and commenced to kick him. The remainder also joined in the kicking, his legs, arms, head and face being injured by the attack. The man Patrick Reed was the ringleader of the prisoners. They ran away when they heard that someone had been sent for assistance, and after being pelted with stones by some young men who had come upon the scene in the meantime. Edward Hankey, Albert Saunders and Win. Hassan, all of Halton, deposed to finding P.C. Juett in a bleeding condition with several of the defendants around him whom they subsequently drove away by throwing stones. The last-named said that as he was accompany- ing another Young m an to Preston Brook for assistance. Joseph Grady threatened to stab him with a clasp knife,</p>
        <p>Microbe</p>
        <p>ral Disinfectant Soap :; a</p>
        <p>From the foregoing experiments it will be seen that the Litebu powerful disinfectant and exterminator of the various germs and—ri-iicr—ob—es of disease. (Signed) KARL ENOCH, Chaß. Hygial. inst., Hamburt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HIGHWAY BOARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this board was held on Wednesday afternoon. Captain T. E. Withington presidea. There were also present :—Messrs. H. Bate, P. I. Bate, Richard Ormrod, James Millington, James Houghton, John Grounds, James Berfoot with Messrs. H. B. I% bite (clerk), E. A. White (deputy clerk), and J. Thompson (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>TENDERS.</head>
        <p>TENDERS.</p>
        <p>Tenders from the following persons were accepted on the motion of Mr. ROYsTON, seconded by Mr. HOIIGHTON. Macadam delivered, Messrs. Brundrit and Co. ; setts, Mr. James Downs; socket pipes, Messrs. Doulton and Co. ; tiles. Messrs. Lewis Birchall, and James Warbur. ton ; cast-iron gullies, Mr. James Smethurst. The ten- ders for spades, shovels and oil were deferred for further consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>MAIN ROARS AGREEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE MAIN ROARS AGREEMENT.</p>
        <p>, • The agreement of the Board with the Lancashire County Council for the maintenance of the main roads by the Board was agreed to. The County Council agree to pay the Board £2,425 for the work, and a special grant of £590, viz: for conversion of boulders to maca- dam £200; kerbing and chanelling Bolton-road £l4O ; and conversion of boulders, Leigh-road £250.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>APPROACH TO HALTON_BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE APPROACH TO HALTON_BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. MILLINGTON said the Board did not seem to get one iota further with the improvement to the road lead- ing tg) Halton Bridge. It appeared as if the inhabitants would have to put up with it for three years more. The Surveyor said he thought the Board would be able to carry out the improvement this autumn. He had made application for the land on the East side of the road,and he expected arrangements would be complete in the course of a week or two. Under the conditions that had been made with the County authorities they were unable to enter on the work until the autumn. THE BOARD'S EXISTENCE._</p>
        <p>Mr. R. ORMROD asked whether it had been decided by the County Council that the Board should continue another three years. The CLERK : We have had no co:n- munication from them. The CHAIRMAN: I suppose we may take it for granted that we are to continue for that time. The CLERK said he wrote a few days ago to the County Council on the matter, but he had not yet had an answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>FINANCE.</head>
        <p>FINANCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK staced that there were accounts outstanding to the amount of £1,647 19s. id., but there was at present only a little over £123 in the bank. There was however over £l,OOO due to them by the County Council, and £826 due on the June call.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>WIDNES SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting was held at the Board Room, Town Hall, on Tuesday afternoon. Mr. H. Wade Deacon, J.P., C.C., presided and there were also present the Revs. Dean Finegan and Father Clarke; Dr. O'Keefe; Messrs. Lewis, Shaw, Brown, Barlow, Wareing, Quinn, Flynn and G. H. Danby (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Committee recommended that a bonus of £3O be given to the Clerk for his valuable services in connection with the Evening Continuation Classes. On this motion being put Messrs. Shaw, Wareing and Brown advocated an increase in the Clerk's salary. He had been a most faithful and painstaking servant to the Board for about sixteen years and since his present salary was fixed his duties had increased very greatly. After a very lengthy discussion Mr. Barlow proposed and Mr. Brown seconded that the Clerk's salary be increased by £5O per annum, but on the same being put to the meeting only five voted in favour of increase and six against. The original motion granting the Clerk the bonus was then put and carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT EDUCATION COMMITTER. This Committee reported that during the month of May there had been five applications for free places, all being granted. There had also been eight prosecutions before the magistrates and eight convictions. On the motion of Father Clarke and Major Wareing the minutes were adopted without discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>NOTICE OF MOTION.</head>
        <p>NOTICE OF MOTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lewis proposed" That re-solution No. 2206 of the proceedings ot the Board be rescinded and that the whole matter of Mr. David Lewis complaints against the School Management Committee be referred to a Special Committee composed of persons not members of this Board for investigation, whose proceedings shall be conducted publicly." In his speech supporting the mo- tion he severely condemned the way in which the special committee had done its work and thought they had not given the matter the attention it deserved.—Messrs. Wareing, Shaw, Quinn, Deacon, and Dean Finegan took part in a very lengthy debate on the matter, and on the motion being put to the meeting only Mr. Lewis voted in its favour all the other members voting against.</p>
        <p>Orders for the steel armour plates required for the two battle-ships to be built in this cßuntry for the Japanese Government have been placed with Sheffield firms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>NEW MEMBER OF THE FIRE ENGINE COMMITTEE. Councillor John Baird has been elected a member of the Fire Engine Committe in plaece of Councillor Muir, resigned. P.S.A. AT THE Y.S.I.C.A. On Sunday last Mr. A. Kent of Leigh, delivered a capital address on "Character Building." It was concise, forcible, and practical and was much en- joyed by the members. A pleasing solo was con- tributed by Miss Cosgrove; Mr. F. Hickman being the accompanist. STARR-BOWKETT APPROPRIATION. The 23rd appropriation meeting was held in the society's office, Cairo-st., on Tuesday night. Mr.W. E. Stout was voted to the chair, and Messrs. Oddie and Ashley were appointed scrutineers. No. 199 was drawn, which entitled Mrs. C. Dean, of Hulme, to £4OO on the usual terms. NEW CURATE AT THE PARISH CHURCH. On Tuesday, the Right Rev. Bishop of Liverpool, who was accompanied by the Ven. Archdeacon Taylor, attended the diocesan registry at Liverpool, and licensed the Rev. Granville Bourdas Thurston, M.A., to the stipendiary curacy of Warrington Parish Church. SANKEY BOARD SCHOOL. Only two members, Messrs. Wright and Bate, put in an appearance at the monthly meeting of the above Board on Tuesday afternoon, in the Board room at the schools. This not being a quorunt, no business was transacted. This is not the first time a similar circumstance has occurred. A letterwas read from Major Ridgway expressing his regret at his inability to be present. ASSAULT ON A WARRINGTON CYCLIST.—HEAVY FINE. James Cheetham, John France, William Flannery, John Shaw, and James Bilborough, all of West Leigh, were summoned at the Leigh Police Court, on Monday, and were each fined 40s. and costs for assaulting William Cleary, a Warrington cyclist. Defendants made an unprovoked attack upon the complainant, kicked him severely, and wrecked his machine. Although they had now paid over .£l6 to Cleary to settle the matter, the magistrates refused to allow the case to be withdrawn. DR. GORNALL AND THE WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. A notice has been sent to each member of the Warrington Board of Guardians by Mr. J. C. Sutton, the clerk, to the effect that at the Board meeting to be held on Wednesday, the 27th inst., Mr. S. Davies will move the following resolution : "That the resolutions passed at the Board meeting on May 30th last upon the subject of determining the appointment of Dr. J. H. Gornall as public vaccinator and of advertising for candidates for the office of public vaccinator, be rescinded, and that the contract • with Dr. Gornall be continued as hithetto." IN'ARRINGTON SUNDAY SCHOOL UNION. In connection with the above-named union a scholars' service was held on Sunday afternoon in the Bold-street Wesleyan Chapel, which was well filled. The preacher was the Rev. Carey Bonner, of Sale, who delivered a very interesting address. His subject was "The Magnet" which he used to il- lustrate the power of Christ to attract people to him, and to hold them safe from the temptations which surrounded them. The rev, gentlemen displaying a rusty nail, shewed by means of several magnets, what a strong one was requisite to take effect upon the nail in consequence of the rust. By this illustration be endeavoured to show his hearers the importance of surrendering themselves to Christ in their youth, before they became incased with the rust of sin. At the conclusion of the address, the president of the union,the Rev.J.W.Varley,of Latch- ford,on behalf of the Sunday School Union thanked the Wesleyan ministers and trustees for their kind- ness in allowing the union the use of the chapel for the service, and also thanked the Rev. Carey Bonner for the interesting and instructive address he had given them. Portions of scripture were read during the service by some of the scholars present. WESLEYAN METHODIST QUARTERLY MEETING. On Monday, the quarterly meeting of the above was held at Bold-street. Tea was provided at 5 p.m. There were present the Revs. J. Kirtlan, Owen Watkins, G. D. Mason, and J. Gunnell and other officials. The tables were presided over by Mrs. W. Bennett, of Padgate, and Mrs. R. G. Henshall, who received the cordial thanks of the ministers and officials for their services. The business of the meeting began at 6.30 p.m. Messrs. W. Bennett and R. G. Henshall (the stewards) presented the financial statement. The income for the quarter had more than met the ex- penditure, but the debt reported three months be- fore was only slightly reduced. It was decided to I ask each place to send the proceeds of the harvest festival, by which help it was hoped that the debt , would be settled. The numerical statement was , very gratifying. The number of members had not decreased for mere than two years. Altogether there were more than 1,300 names on the class books, ' exclusive of cemmunicants who are not tabulated. The Sunday School report was presented by the Rev.G.D.Mason. The number of scholars was 2,777, of which 819 were above 15 years of age. In the Band of Hope there are 1,179 members. The Rev. W.G. Triggs, of Ridhill circuit, was unanimously in- vited to take the place of third minister for 1895. A very cordial invitation to remain another year was accepted by the Rev. J. Gunnell. Arrangements are being made for the opening of the new church at Latchford,by the president of the conference,and the ex-president. the Rev. J. Grange Bennett, &amp;c., on October 11th and following Sundays. GOOD TEMPLARY AT LATCHFORD. On Monday evening, a lodge session was held in the Temperance Hall, conducted solely by the sis- ters. The officers were as follows :—Chiet ternplar, Sister A. Rough; vice templar, J. Scott; past chief templar, A. Ellison; chaplain, J. Hampton; secre- tary, L. Hough ; assistant secretary, J. Edwards ; financial secretary, M. Fletcher; marshal, A. Highly; deputy marshal, A. Dawson; guard, A. Walker: sentinel, E. Dawson. After the usual business of the lodge had been gone through. the following pro- gramme was presented :—Opening song, "Break the pledge, never"; trio, Siste s Scott, Edwards and Fletcher; recitation, Sister L. Hough ; song. Sister A. Dawson; recitation, Sister J.: Hampton; reading, Sister A. Highly; song, Sister M. Fletcher; recitatioJ, Sister 3. Scott; duet, Sisters Dawson and Highly; reading, Sister A. Hough ; song, Sister J. Scott. During the evening oranges were distributed. It was proposed by Bro. L. Ellison. seconded by Bro. E. Cliffe, and supported by Bros. H. Richardson, A. Ford and J. Boscow, that the best thanks be given to the sisters. Sister A. Hough, chief ternplar, suitably responded. The session was closed by singing and prayer.</p>
      </div>
