      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>TRE EXAMINERS</head>
        <p>TRE EXAMINERS</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. JUNE 23. 1894.</p>
        <p>tbe and the THE Leeds Conference of dole- -80 3 t'se of Lords gates of the National Liberal lead , • Federation has given a strong ti 40 the Government on the question of the pLII4 of Lords. A conflict between the Liberal iY and the House of Lords had clearly become theovi,table before this conference was called, and ti;°.ruject of this great gathering of represents- -114 Überals was not merely to give any further elissarY emphasis to that fact, but really to the upon a "plan of campaign." Either her:itarY Mending " or the " ending " of the Chamber has to be accomplished -ne way or another, and no better method ;t, ascertaining the prevailing opinion of ressive ta:ed th an party in the country could be de- that of holding a conference of ropre- ofiltittives. Delegates were present from all parts t:ingland and Wales. Cornwall, one extreme of vet, la", and Northumberland, the other extreme, thee alike represented, to say nothing of ot intermediate counties. The Albert Hall Leeds was packed with about two thou- delegates. The most ardent enthusiasm at wtl disPlaYed and the decisions were arrived practical unanimity. What difference of fencitil°o there was did not arise between de- ll e,r3 and opponents of the House of Lords. reera had no champions in this gathering, as, TA", they erals cannot have now-a-dais where are con grehated together. The line of Thli,svage was between " menders " and "coders." kr: resolution that was submitted to the Con- Wastl *worded as follows :—" That this meet- C 4113 upon the Government to introduce a 'e t 0 for the abolition of the House of Lords' th:' bY providing that whenever a Bill, passed by jeet.„l:l°Use of Commons, shall be altered or re- ramp' in the House of Lords, such Bill eon,' be re-approved by the House of atld.llloss, with or without such alteration, epo' an beet only to the Royal assent, shall there- become law." It wili be seen that this itlCtition proposes to restrict, and indeed, to all 110-,,11ts and purposes, to abolish the veto of the of 1:48 of Lords. If it were acted upon the House it, „;°r,dB Would, as one of the speakers aptly put 'linto "Picturesque decay." It would still titilla to meet, to debate, and, in appearance,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>to" maim and mutilate" in the old well-under- stood way, the popular measures submitted to it. But it would only have one opportunity of doing so, and its triumph would be extremely short, lived. On the Bill going back to the House of Commons that Assembly might amend all the ,amendments of the Lords according to its own desires, and the Bill would then be passed on, over the heads of the Peers, for the Royal assent after receiving which, it wouli become law. A House of Lords with its powers for mischief limited in this fashion would be tolerable. The I resolution was, however, not drastic enough for some of those who attended the Conference. Mr. LABOUCHERE is a sworn enemy of hereditary legislation, and he proposed an amendment which affirmed that the" House of Lords is useless, I dangerous, and ought to be abolished," and, with this object in view, urged the Government to in- troduce a measure for the abolition of the veto in the present session of Parliament. The temper of the Conference may be gauged by the fact that the amendment received a large amount of support, and although it was not adopted in its entirety, a clause was added to the resolution calling upon the Government to introduce a Bill during the present Parliament. It can, therefore, now be said that the Government have received a strong and significant mandate from the general body of the Liberal party. This is what Lord ROSEBERY asked for, and it may be expected that he and his colleagues will act upon it.</p>
        <p>THE Duke of DEVONSHIRE made A Landlord's an unwise speech at Buxton the Threat. other day. The Duke is one of the very wealthiest men in England. He is the landlord not only of extensive estates, but of whole villages and even towns, and, consequently, his rent-roll attains almost fabulous dimensions. Yet, wealthy as he is, and great as are the interests in which ho is secured by the State, he is one of the loudest of those who cry out against the mild proposals for graduating taxation which are now before Parliament, and so far as we know, he is the first great landowner who has, in effect, threatened his tenantry that the additional imposts on his overgrown fortunes shall be made to fall on their shoulders. Ho inti- mates that if the present budget passes, "great changes will have to be made in the administration, of his estates." Hitherto, we take it, the connection between landlord and tenant has been largely intermixed with senti- ment, but in future the relationship will be on a purely business basis. He claims that from thirty to seventy per cent. of the revenues derived from the Devonshire properties have been locally expended, but, if there be any meaning in his throats, in time to come the Dukes of DEVON- SHIRE will follow the methods and policy of ab- sentee landlords. It is hard to see what end the Duke of DEVONSHIRE expects to serve by language of this sort. It will most assuredly not weaken the determination of the Government and the coun- try that the present Budget proposals shall become law. What cannot be won by argument and rea- on will not he obtained by threats. The Duke is certainly not promoting the interests of the class of landlords to which he belongs. Absentee landlordism of the Irish type will not be tolerated in England. And by displacing the sentimental ties which still exist between landlord and tenant in many of the rural parts of England the Duke will help in a very effectual way to bring down the system to which he almost entirely owes his own social and political import- ance. The present land system of England has not been built on business lines. It is a system which is throwing almost whole counties out of cultivation, and under which, in a progressive age, agriculture languishes and declines. By carrying out the policy which ho seems to meditate the Duke of DEVONSIIIRE will become one of the most potent allies of the extreme land reformers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>FAIINWORTII remsa CHURCEL</p>
        <p>Special sermons were preached in the above church on Sunday in aid of the Parish Church day schools. The Rev. S. A. B. Sylvester' M.A., of Roby, preached in the morning, and the Rev. F. B. Tyrer, M.A., of Liverpool, in the evening. In the afternoon an address to the children was delivered by Mr. Tyrer. There were good congregations and the collections reached a fair SUM. COLLIER'S sell the best quality goods at lowest possible prices.—. Advt. 'FRE ETON MEMORIAL TO MR. T. C. EDWARDS-MOSS.</p>
        <p>At a representative gathering of supporters of" The Edwards-Moss Memorial Fund," held last week, Dr. Warre, head master of Eton, in the chair, it was resolved that a sum should be invested in the names of the trmtees of the Eton Mission, Hackney-wick, feT the purpose of enabling convalescents from that district to obtain tickets of admission to certain hospitals free of cost. It was proposed that the balance should be expended either on coloured glass for a window in the Lower Chapel at Eton College or on some other perman- ent form of memorial at the college.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S FRESH IRISH DAIRY BUTTER, 10d. per lb is excellent.— Advt.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARIES AT _WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Tin anniversary services in connection with the United Methodist Free Church Sunday School, were held on Sunday and Monday. On Sunday morning and evening sermons were preached by the Rev C. Pyc to large congregations. In the afternoon, a service of song entitled " Adrift," was admirably rendered by the scholars and iriendo. Councillor Owens presided and Miss Gardner read the narrative in an interesting n-,anner. On Monday evening a miscellaneous programme was gone through, and greatly enjoyed. Collections were taken at each service, in aid of the school funds, and amounted to £23 12s.—The anniversary in connec- tion with the Ditton Wesleyan Sunday School was cele- brated on Sunday. The Rev. R. Odery (fomerly minis- ter of the Widnes circuit) preached in the morning and evening to good congregations and in the afternoon Mr. F. Dodd of Widnes delivered an interesting address to teachers, scholars and friends. The collections realised about £l2.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S sell everything for the smallett possib:e profit.— Advt.</p>
        <p>RUFCORN LIBRARY COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of this committee was held en Tuesday evening. in the Boardroom, at the Town Hall, Runcorn, Mr.- W. Handley occupying the chair.. There were also present the Rev. John Simp- s.m, Messrs. H.J. Grim W. Hankinson, Thomk's Riley, and J. D. Jones (librarian).—The Chairman on behalf of the committee extended a hearty welcome to the Rev. J. Simpson, who had been elected to fill the vacancy caused through Mr. NV. M. Dowerby leaving the town. The rev, gentleman would, the Chairman remarked, assist them very materially in the choice of books for the library.—The Rev. J. Simpson in acknowledgement said he thanked them very much for the honour they had conferred upon him, and expressed his intention of .always discharging the duties appertaining to his posi- tion to the best of his ability.—The Librarian reported that the receipts durin, the month amounted to £5l 10,. al., which included £5O transferred to them by the C)minissioners from the Library rate. After deducting the expenditure they had a balance in hand of £3l 0. 4d. The books issued for the month numbered 1,833 from the lending department, and 27 from the reference department. The former number was made no as follows :—Theology, 20 ; philosophy, 9 ; science 65 ; history, 134 ; poetry,l9 : fiction, 1,428 ; miscella- neous, 161. Compared with last year there was a de- crease in the honks of reference of 16-: wtils 3e the other department there was an increase of la.—The re- port was adopted and the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 23, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 23, 1894.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN GROCER AND HIS CREDITORS.</p>
        <p>The " Gazette " of luasday night announces that a first meeting of the creditns of John Stringer, grocer, Runcorn, will take place on the 26% inst. at Ogden's Chambers, Manchester, at 3 o'clock in the afternoon ; and an examination on the 6th July at the County Court, Warrington, at 11 o'clock.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S BLACK BLENDED TEA, 20. per quarter lb.—Advt. ROBBERY FROM A WIDNES BOARD SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Thursday at the Widnes Petty Sesions, before Messrs. Pilkington and J. Stone, Sarah Jane Kinsey, aged 11, of Millbrow, and Edith Peel, aged 8, of Carlisle- f•t., were charged with stealing a cloak from Siimns Cross Board School, on Monday afternoon. Kinsey was also ehafged with stealing a jackE.t from the Warring- ton-road Board School, in May last. Kinsey pleaded guilty and was fieed 103., but Peel was dismissed with a caution.</p>
        <p>OUTING FROM RUNCORN TO EASTHAM.</p>
        <p>About three hundred mem oers aril associates of the Runcorn Branch cf the Girls' Friendly Society had a most enjoyable outing on Thursday to Eastham. Assem- bling at the Pier Head, Ituncprn, at about one o'clock some two hundred embarked on board the Manx Fairy and steamed to Weston Point where another party was taken on board. The clergymen who accompanied the excursionists were the Revs Canon:Wood, vicar of Run- corn; d. J. Jameson, W. H. Stables, and H. B. Firth. They then continued their journey along the Canal t., Eastliana which place was reached at about two o'clock. The vicar of Eastham's grounds had been thrown open for the enjoyment and the inspection of them was a source of much pleasure. They afterwards congregated in the church were a short service was conducted subsequent to which tea was par- taken of. The party then broke up into groups and rambled at leisure through the gardens and amused themselves in various ways. Beautiful weather pre- vailed and the excursion was a great success, everyone agreeing that the afternoon had been one of the most pleasant they had spent for a long time. The return journey was began at half-past seven o'clock, arriving at Runcorn at nine. CROFT UNITARIAN SCHOOL TEA. PARTY.</p>
        <p>On Monday the annual tea party for the Sunday scholars, teachers, choir, and friends of the Unitar- ian Chapel was held in the schoolroom. Afterwards a number of games were engaged in in a field lent for the occasion by Mr. Welsby, and nuts and sweets were distributed amongst the children. SEWING MACHINES Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handtome walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, Sankey-st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>CROFT UNITARIAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. On Sunday afternoon and evening the anniver- sary sermons on behalf of the Sunday school were preached in the Unitarian Chapel, Lady-lane, by the Rev. u McGa.vin Sloan, of Hamilton-road Chapel, Liverpool. There were large congregations, friends corning from Warrington, Leigh, A.stley, and other places. The choir of Cairo-st. Chapel, Warrington, kindly gave their services. The anthems, "No Shadows Yonder," in the afternoon, and "0 love the Lord" (Sullivan), in the evening, were admirably rendered. Mr. James Muir was at the organ, and his manipulation of the instrument was much ap- preciated. The chapel and schools had been recently painted and decorated for the occasion. SOAKING RAIN PROOF OVERCOATS, yet porous. 559., C. JACKSON !lOWANS. General Outfitter, Market Gate, IVarriniz- tou.—Adrt.</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN Al RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>To all appearances, the question of providing. a graving dock at Runcorn, by the Manchester Snip Canal Company, is becoming more and more im- portant every day. The deputation of painters, shipwrights, &amp;c., recently received by the Commis- sioners, to hear an expression of opinion upon the matter, exhaustively laid bare their views, and did so in such a masterly manner as to remove many doubts from the minds of certain Commissioners who were rather inclined to think that a graving dock would not pay at Runcorn. Of course, the Commissioners could do nothing more than patiently hear the statements made by various members of the deputation, and in return to give their assur- ances that the situation should be very carefully considered. The alternative scheme of the Ship Canal Company has, as yet, not been received by the law clerk, so that the General Purposes Committee, practically cannot advance or retreat, as it were, but have to remain stationary. The sooner some- thing in the foem of a definite proposal is laid be- fore the Committee by the Company the better.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel Taylor has once more been chosen chairman by his fellow Commissioners, and if he only performs his onerous duties in the same efficient manner in the future as in past, they will certainly have no reason to regret their choice. Mr. Taylor displayed an unselfish siiirit in offering to second the appointment to the chair of Mr. Prit- chard, an old member of the Board. This gentle- man, however, manifested the same spirit as Mr. Taylor, explaining that he coal I not see his way to monopolise the chair of the School Board, and also that of the Commissioners. He therefore supported the election of Mr. Taylor, which was agreed %lon with unanimity.</p>
        <p>The question raised by Mr. Pritchard, though out of order at that meeting, was an important one both to the inhabitants of Runcorn and Widnes. The need of a proper landing stage and fixed times at which passenger steamers would stop at Runcorn, has, since the opening of the Canal,been sorely felt. The wonder is that the company has not recog- nised the want,seeing that there is so much chance of developing a good traffic at this point. As it was remarked by Mr. Pritchard,if the stage was erected where the old ferry slip used to be, it would prove extremely convenient, not only for passengers from Runcorn, but also from Widnes,who wished to take advantage of the Canal for a sail to Liverpool or Manches'..er. Messrs. Speakman and Taylor will surely have the wishes of numberless folks for their success in attaining the required provision.</p>
        <p>An object deserving the sympathy of the public, and more particularly of seamen, is that of con- structing a grave in the Runcorn Cemetery for the interment of unfortunate sailors who are drowned away from home. The object is one, which Mr. Wm. Shaw, the local missionary to seamen, has worked for during the last two or three years, but has only just succeeded in procuring the consent of his committee at Liverpool to carry it out. The scheme is to purchase a plot of land sufficiently large for the interment of twenty coffins containing the remains of seamen drowned in the locality. .In the past these sailors have been buried in the poor ground, and no stone or anything else erected to state' who were beneath. Relatives and friends of these poor fellows have sometimes heard of their decease and have travelled to Runcorn with the express purpose of seeing where their bodies were buried, and their disappointment may be imagined better than described when told that the spot could not be found. It has been decided to erect a handsome monument over the grave upon which will be inscribed the names of those who may be buried beneath whenever they are known. It will be called "The sailors' grave," and it is esti- mated that the total cost of the ground,monument, S.:(!. will be about £3O, which Mr. Shaw is trying to raise by subscriptions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>DEATH OF A RUNCORN EX-CONSTABLE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A RUNCORN EX-CONSTABLE.</p>
        <p>The death is announced as having taken place on the 12th inst. at Upholland, Wigan, of James Wilson Thom- son, in his forty-sixth year. It will be remembered that about nine months ago the deceased left Runcorn through illness, having served ten years as a constable at the Old Quay, and one at Runcorn. He was well-known in the town, and was a very useful officer, always discharging his duty in a satisfactory manner. He was unmarried and at the time of his death was residing with his brother.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LIVERPOOL PUBLICAN FINED AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>A LIVERPOOL PUBLICAN FINED AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at the Widnes Petty Sessions, before Messrs. G. Pilkington and J. Stone, John James Ramsey, of the Sandon Hotel, Liverpool, was summoned for using a number of unstamped measures. It appears that the defendant was the holder of an occasional license on the sports day at Farnworth on the 9th inst., and on Inspector Kilpatrick making an inspection of the glasses he found about 120 half pint glasses unstamped. The glasses were not short measure. A tine of 10s. and 123. costs was imposcid.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING PRESENTATION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Hankinson has just recently been made the re- cipient of an elegantly bound comprehensive Bible as a token of esteem and an af:knowledgement of earnest labour whilst connected with the Runcorn Trinity Church, and the Young Mens' Bible Class. The Rev. Dr. Preston, vicar of Holy Trinity, made the Presenta- tion, and referred in terms of eulogism to the eiestities of Mr. H. Hankinson whom he had known for 22 years. Mr. Hankinson returned his thanks for the testimonial in a short but appropriate address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WIDNES SAILOR DROWNED AT SEA.</head>
        <p>A WIDNES SAILOR DROWNED AT SEA.</p>
        <p>Captain Mußinger, of the schooner" Harvest Rin," of Runcorn, who has recently returned from a voyage to Falmouth, brings the following particulars of the death of an able-bodied seaman, engaged on his vessel, and who is a native of Widnes. About nine o'clock on the morning of the 6th June, when in Broad Sound, Milford Haven, and returning to Runcorn,Thomas Stevens,about 21 years of age, whose family lived in Victoria-road, Widnes. accidently fell from the upper top-sail yard, overboard into the sea. A life buoy was immediately thrown to him, and every possibleezertion made to save his life,but as he was unable to swim these efforts proved fruitless. Intelligence has been forwarded to his rela- tives in Widnes, who were much shocked at his unex- pected death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORNIANS AT DELAMERE FOREST,</head>
        <p>RUNCORNIANS AT DELAMERE FOREST,</p>
        <p>On Monday morning the teachers, choir, a few scholars and friends of the Runcorn Branch of the Mersey Mission to Seamen had an excursion to Delatn.re Forest. They assembled at the institute and began their journey at about eleven o'clock in wagg43nettes. The party numbered about 40 and on starting out they were favoured with fine weather. Later on, however, rain fell and in some measure marred th 6 enjoyment of the participants. On arriving at Eddisbury Lodge they alighted and rambled through the forest. Afterwards they partook of a capital tea, prepared by the ladies,and subsequently indulged in amusements of many kinds. At length the excursionists returned home which was reached at ten o'clock, having spent, under the circum- Et4nces, a pleasant day.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLISTS: RUNS TO BALE AND LY.15114.</p>
        <p>The destination of the Saturday riders of the Runcorn Cycling Club was Hale,and at 2.30 prompt they started in strong force from Devonshire-square. Runcorn, and took the route through Warrington and Wootton. They dismounted at the Lighthouse where they indulged in a tasty tea and enjoyed themselves until nine o'clock, when they returned home all agreeing that it had been one of the most pleasant outings they had experienced. The Wednesday members of the club ran to Lymm. The weather was sc disagreeable at 2.30 on Wednesday afternoon that the run was postponed until 5..30, when an assembly was once more made in Devonshire-square, and the journey to Lymm was begun. The roads were very heavy and the runners found the running conse- quently hard. Nevertheless, they were determined that the run should not be abandoned,and pressed on through Moore and turned off at Walton, arriving at Lymm at seven o'clock. Here they amused themselves in various ways, and subsequently returned to Runcorn,which was reached in good time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>RAILWAY FATALITY AT NVIDNES.</head>
        <p>RAILWAY FATALITY AT NVIDNES.</p>
        <p>About 7.30 on Tuesday morning an accident occurred to a foreman platelayer, named Thomas Platt, on the St. Helens and Widnes line of the London and North- Western Railway Company near Widnes station. It appears that two coal trains were about to pass each other an in stepping out of the way of the up train Platt was caught by the down train, and dragged along for a great distance beneath the fire-box. On being extricated from his perilous position it was found that no bones were broken, but the unfortunate fellow was terribly bruised about the head and body. He was removed to the Accident Hospital and attended to by Dr. Hutchinson. He died shortly after admission.—On Wednesday afternoon at the Widnes Accident Hospital, Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy county coroner, held an inquiry touching the death and after hearing the evidence the jury returned a verdict of " Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PROPOSED PUBLIC BATHS FOR RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED PUBLIC BATHS FOR RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Committee appointed to consider this subject is still working vigorously. On Wednesday afterngon the sub-committee, consisting of Messrs. William Handley, Thomas Lea, and William Leach, in company with Mr. M. Barker, surveyor, visited the Runcorn Waterworks, on the Common. It had been snggested that the cool- ing pond, attached to the works, which measures more than one hundred square yards, might be utilized as a temporary bath seeing that the water is nearly always tepid and was of a uniform depth of five fe et six inches. The object of the Committee's visit can therefore be seen, and it is understood that after having inspected the pond they were of the opinion that it would be a favourable site for a temporary bath if it was boarded round and wooden booths erected. A meeting of the Baths Com- mittee will shortly be held when the sub-comniittee will report to them the result of their investigations and pro- bably recommend them to make the necessary additions and improvements to the cooling pond with the above mentioned view. The Committee will then consider the matter and at the next meeting of Commissioners may report something of a definite character. It is estimated that the cost of hoarding the pond round, and erecting the wooden cabins, would be comparatively trifling, and the stork could be completed in a very short time.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S BREAD is the best money can buy.—Advt. THE BIINCORN BIGAMY CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Chester Assizes on Saturday held at Chester Castle bofoTe Mr. Justice Vaughan Williams Mary Dag- nall (26) pleaded guilty to committing a bigamy with a Mersey Waterman namod Jesse Duckers at Runcorn Parish Church, on April 4,1894. Jesse Duckers was also indicted for aiding and abetting Duman in the bigamy. Mr. E. Burke Wood prosecuted in the case of Duckers, and from his statement of the facts of the ease it ap- peared that the female prisoner was married in 1855 to William Dagnall, her maiden name being Mary Beckett, at Runcorn Parish Church.. William Dagnall was then employed on a fiat, Dockers being similary emnloyed, hut in a more subordinate position to Dagnall. Eighteen months ago Mrs. Dagnall left her husband, and subse- quent to that she became intimate with Duckers,and the two eventually went through the form of marriage at Runcorn Parish Church.—Several witnesses were called, some of whom spoke to the first marriage being con- tracted,and thers to seeing the second ceremony.—The Judge, in summing up, said it was an unusual kind of bigamy,the offence being one more in theory than in actual fact. The woman had left her first husband anti been living intimately with Puckers before the bigamy took place.—The jury returned a verdict of "Not guilty" against Dockers, who was acquitted, whilst the female prisoner, who said her first. husband had married her under a wrong name and had been very cruel to- wards her, was sent to gaol for three weeks.</p>
        <p>COLLIER'S is. BUTTER is th 3 gem in the market.—Advt. DRINK AND WRETCHEDNESS AT Wu:IN:Es.</p>
        <p>On Thursday, at the Widnes Petty Sessions, before Messrs. G. Pilkington and J. Stone, a labourer named Frank Hunter, 5, Charlotte-stseet,and his wife,Elizabeth Hunter, were summoned at the instance of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children for systematically neglecting their three children, Mary Ellen, aged seven, Florence, aged five, and Francis Joseph, aged three, in such a manner as to cause them unnecessary pain and suffering between February and the 9th inst. Mr. Bucktnn, of Warrington, vho prose- cuted, said both the defendants were well-known to the court. The were both frequently drunk—three or four nights a week. The three children were insured.—ln- spector M'Ateer stated that he visited the defendants' house on the 9th of the present month, and found 0,, three children alone in the hack kitehen. They were very dirty and filthy. There was no fire, and not a particle of food in the cupboard. On a bench was an old flock, which served as a bed. At noon on the 10th inst. he again visited the house, and found the children in the same condition.—Police-sergeant Fretwell, who visited the house with Inspector M'Ateor, corroborated that officer's statement—Dr. O'Keefie said he saw the three children on the afternoon of the 9,,h. They were clean, hut the house was dirty—The bench said thoy veenia a to 5.,) fter attend to r,h.r ,Inl:lren in a proper manner. If they did the society might overlook the matter. The case was then adj,yarued until July Mb.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>HOMING PIGEONS FOR FRANCE.</head>
        <p>HOMING PIGEONS FOR FRANCE.</p>
        <p>The West Lancashire Saturday Federation despatched on Thursday morning, from Lime-street station, Liverpool, 1,071 pigeons belonging to the fetierattd clubs, and some non-members birds from places as far distant as Swin4lon, Wakefield, and Millom, for Cherbourg. The birds were contained in sixty-five baskets, and looked very comfortable eating their corn and drinking water on the plet- form, where they caused considerable attraction and interest. The toss will take place early on Sat- urday morning. Mr. John Jolley, of St. Helens is conveyor-in-charge, and will travel with the birds, via Willesden and Southampton. Mr. H. J. Longton president of the federation,made the French declar- ation on Thursday morning, before the Lord Mayor of Liverpool and the French Consul. SIMING MAcnrsEs. Hand £2 18s. 63. Treadle 24 4s. wl'h handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, Sank ey-s t. Warrington.— Ativt THE FARMERS STCHIES.—ITS CAPITAL. Mr. Hatton (Messrs. R. Davies and C 0.,) made an application before His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes at the Warrington County Court on Thurs- day morning, under the Companies Act of 1867, on behalf of the Lancashire and Cheshire Farmers' Association for the confirmation of the reduction of its capital. This is in all probability the first ap- plication of the kind that has been brought before any County Court, it being generally understood that only the High Court can deal with such matters. It is believed, however, that the Com- panies Winding-up Act of 1890 confers jurisdiction upon County Courts. His Honour stated that he would reserve his decision until the.next coutt day, in order that he might have time to enter thoroughly into the case. In consequence of the application, the ordinary business of the court was not proceeded with until half past eleven.</p>
        <p>SEWING MACHINES, Haul £2 183. 6d. Tr.,dle £4 43. with handsoino walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, -Bankey-st., Warrington —Advt. GOOD TEMPLAR'S. On Wednesday evening upwards of 35 members of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars, with a few friends, journeyed to Warrington, to pay a visit to the Temperance Mission Lodge meeting, in the mission room, Academy-street. The visitors arrived at their destination about 7.45, and were heartily welcomed. After the formal business was trans- acted they were invited to occupy the officer, chairs. Bro. Joseph Anderton, C.T., presiding. A : few opening remarks from the chairman, were fol- lowed by an excellent programme,every item being i rendered in a very appreciative manner. Solos were given by Sisters Mrs. Willis and Airs. Wakefield, Bros'. 0. Edwards and W. Hallett. Recitations by , Bro. George E. Warbutton and Win. Wilson, and Sister Miss A. Newman. An appropriate reading was given by Bro. W. Blair. A choir numbering 15 voices rendered three pieces very nicely, Bro. Geo. Clarke conducting. and Bro. W. Willis accompanied on the harmonium. There was a large audience, and thanks were heartily accorded the visitors for their encouraging programme. Refreshments were afterwards banded round. Gai.7 BALLS.—GARLIND Ironmonger, Warrington and Earlestown.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RECONSTRUCTION OF CELEBRATED WORKS AT BOLD.</head>
        <p>RECONSTRUCTION OF CELEBRATED WORKS AT BOLD.</p>
        <p>A start has been made to reconstruct the old works at Bold, and very shortly a new industry— viz„ the manufacture of steel tubes, will be com- menced. The works in question were erected about thirty-five years slum by the St. Helens Smelting Company, and in regard to the emission of noxious vapours from them. the late Mr. William Tipping, of Bold Hall. obtained an injunction. An appeal was made to the House of Lords in 1865. with the result that the injunction was made absolute, and the works were closed. They then appear to have fallen into the bands of a firm of patent manure manufacturers, whii carried on their business for some years. For a considerable period now the ex- tensive works have been standing idle and falling into decay, and a disastrous gale early in the year assisted largely in the work of demolition. Mr. J. Wharton, of King-street. Manchester, estate agent, has just concluded negotiations with Messrs.Robert- ' son and Co„ engineers, of that city, and the works have changed hands. A number of men under Mr. Davies, of Preston, are engaged in pulling down huge brick walls and arches, in which operation, roburite has to be employed, and they are also en- gaged in the .risky business of taking off roofs which show unmistakable signs ot collapse. Mr. William Eden, of Sutton, is engaged getting the railway sidings in order. We understand that nego- tiations are in progress with a local contractor for the erection of new sheds, and that in about four months a start in the new manufacture will prob- ably be made. As showing the condition of the i labour market t may he stated that when it was first intimated that these works would probably be reconstructed several hundreds of men from various parts, including South Wales, flocked to the place only to find disappointment awaiting them. The alterations will not require a large num- ber of men,but the industry itself will be gradually developed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WIDNES PETIY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES PETIY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Bernre Messrs. G. Pilkington and J. Stone, FINED FOR DRUNKENNESS.—Patrick Burn, Grove-st., and Walter Clarke, Grove-st. CHIMNEY ON FIRE- --Ann Rogers, Waterloo-road. was lined is. and 4s. costs for having the chimney of her dwelling-house on fire. UNMUZZLED Doos.—Susannah Tickle, Lacev-st— ; James Evans, Mersey-road ; Margaret Dixon, Water- road ; Thomas Bagridge, Gladstone-st. ; James Percival, Moorfield-road ; and William Rowlands, Mersey-road, were fined 55. and costs for allowing their dogs to be at large without being securely muzzled. Scßoor, ATTENDANCE SUMMONSES.—Mar Morley, 19, Viaduct-street, and George Fludson, 25, Victoria-street, were summoned for not sending their boys to school regularly. In each case an order was made for the boys to be sent to the Ilightown Truants' School. Matthew Caffey, 68, Pear-street, and Michael Devereux, 44, Vic- toria-street, were each fined 61. and 45. 61. costs for the above offence.—Patrick Flt z3iinmons, I iike-street,Ditton, was fined ed. and 4s. 63. costs for neglecting to send his children regularly to school. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—James Knight, 23, James- street, was fined 53. and 65. 6,1. costs for being drunk and disorderly.</p>
        <p>THROwINO .STONES.—Richard Ratcliffe, Church-street, was fined 2. 6d. and 7s. 61. costs for throwing stones in a public place, to the common danger. -• UNMUZZLED DOG. Michael Bennett, Appleton. was fined 10s. and 7s. 6d. costs for not having his dog seetirely !nuzzled. DEFRAUDING TUE LONDON AND NORTII-WESTERN RAIL- WAY COMPANY.—Florence Pach-nore. a widow, about 35 9, Chnim "Vi,InPR, was charged with rielina on Of. Taondon and Norh-Western Railway ,ithour, having' paid a sufficient fare. Thafendant was fined 203. and 3.4.'. costs, or one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>EXTRAOFDINARY DEATH OF A flOaSE.—At about four o'clock on Sunday afternoon, a valuable horse belonging to Mr. Albert Wilson, of Frodshani, and which had been placed in a field near Frodsham Bridge, was discovered to be seriously wounded in two places. At first its owner thought that it had been stabbed by some mali- cious person or persons, but upon a veterinary surgeon In tking an examination of the animal, lie formed the idea that it had either been gored by one of the cows in the kid, or had run against a point ,d stake, and thus indicted the injury npnn itself. The wounds were so dangerous that the animal required continual attention, but notwithAanding the treatment of the veterinary surgeon it died the cam- night from the injuries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>TRAP ACCIDENT.—An accident, which might have culminated in the loss of life, occurred on Sunday morning at Halton. Mr. Williams of the Bridgewater Arms, Runcorn, accompanied by Miss Burns and his children were returning home from a drive, and on reaching the brow of the hill which leads down from Halton village to Runcorn, the traces by some means became unfastened. The noise of the loose traces start- led the animal which ran at a high speed down the hill, upsetting the trap. The occupants, who were thrown with much force to the ground, were injured, Mr. Wil- liams sustaining a serious wound at the back of his head. One of the children was hurt internally and Miss Burns was badly marked about the face. Dr. McLennan at- tended them, and on Thursday they were recovering satisfactorily. TEMPERANCE MERTING.—On Wednesday evening the annual meeting of the Halton Wesleyan adult Temper- ance Society was held when a large gathering was pre- sent. The Rev. J. Hanby filled the chair, and addresses were given by the Rev. F. Godson, of the West Indies, Mr. D. Bisbrown, Runcorn; and Miss Bessie Dutton, of Moore. The subject of the speeches was the success of the temperance cause, and the benefits it conferred upon many of its advacates.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>GREYT BON-VIN' MATCIT!</head>
        <p>A GREYT BON-VIN' MATCIT!</p>
        <p>Come on, owd Pen, an' go to wark, Its gettin' welly time ; Its not so far off twelve months sin' Theau did sum docent rhyme. We'll sing abeaut that Broughton Club, Wo fowt us oncet agen ; An' smilin', took ther leatherid, Like good an' Christian men. We fost sent on eaur Grand Owd Mon, Wi' a Yeauth fro' Macclesfield; An' soon seed mischief wur abeaut, Per booath on urn peel'd. Like two owd Trojans these two fowt, To be on th' winnin' side ; An starnly show'd thoose Broughton chaps Thay wouldn't be denied. Then th' gallant Captain geet agate, Wi'Nery sarious looks; For he'd the very dule to meet.— Immortal Brother Brooks !" nay struggle't like true Britons, too, Fer which shud be ith' front, An' didn't, know until th' lost eend, Which lied to do a shunt." " Eaur Jim," an' " Lisha," smolt: a smile, Thay'd such a soft thing cn ; It wud ha' takken t'other pair To settlesaither mon ! An owd friend " Tommy " hed his turn, So lied the faithful " Nunn ;" But truth to tell, an' sad to say, Thay both geet badly done. But, thoose two Peters!! oh! by Gyow ! They made me fairly smook ; One geet quite wild, an' swore becoze His pardner cudn't flook ! Wile t'other nautter'd to hissel', "This is a gradely ; These hloomin' snowbo.ws connot see A bow's not quite a cue!" To mention aw eaur teams wud tak' A greyt deal too much space : But noon en um look'd sad or deawn, They'd aw a smilin' face. Aw've Myles to go yet, hnt 1 cautft, Aw'm run reet off my lees • Beside, aw'm modest, so aw'll stop.— Yore's truly, TIT3I O'PEGS, Rose Bonk, 20, 6, '94.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>STREET ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>STREET ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>About six o'clock -on Satur.fav night, a horse attached to a trap which was standing outside the provision shop of Messrs. Charles Wilcock and Son, Baldwin-street, Sr. Flelens, shied at a steam tram engine coming from Denton's Green, and backed the trap into the window. A largo sheet of plate glass was broken, but although the street was thronged at the time no persons were injured.</p>
