      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>INSPECTION OF THE 6TH N.B.K,L.R.</p>
        <p>The annual inspection of the above battalion took place at Sefton Park, on Saturday. The Newton detachment numbering 126 left Newton for Liver- pool by train at, 2.50. On arrival at Lime-st. they proceeded to headquarters, 59, Everton-road. The inspecting officer, Colonel Eyre Williams, commanding the Bth and 40th regimental district, arrived at the headquarters shortly before four o'clock and examined the books, arms, and stores, and expressed himself thoroughly well satisfied with the cleanliness and order I everywhere apparent, as also with the separate accomo- dation for the officers, sergeants, corporals and privates. The battalion, under the command of Lieutenant-Col. Wood, mustered at 4.30 p.m. and were joined at head- quarters by the Newton detachment. Sefton Park was reached about six o'clock and after the companies had been sized and equalized, line was formed, and the in- specting officer was received with the general salute. Accompanied by Lieutenant-Colonel Wood, Colonel Eyre Williams rode down the ranks, and narrowly inspected the clothing and accoutrements of the men, afterwards inspecting the bands, cyclists, and ambulance. Several evolutions were then gone through, but by request of the inspecting officer,the manual and firing and bayonet exercises were not gone through in order that more time might be devoted to the " attack," but all the captains present were called out in succession and exercised the battalion in various movements. Lieutenant-Colonel Wood resumed command, and after the " attack " had been gone through as well as the limited space at dis- posal would permit, quarter column was again fOrmed, and the inspection was at an end. Addressing the battalion,Colonel Eyre-Wiliiams said he was extremely well pleased with all he had seen that day. The clothing and accoutrements of all ranks were clean and well put on and the men had a smart and soldier-like appearance. He had also noticed that a common fault with volunteers had been conspicuous by its absence—the men had remained steady in the ranks and had not moved and talked and followed with their eyes the inspecting officer. He had been with them a week or two before at the Mersey Defence Mobilization Scheme, when they had made that forced march to the Docks Station in such an exceedingly short spate of time. The embarkation and disembarkation were con- ducted in a most orderly manner and not a moment's time was lost at any stage of the journey. That march alone showed him that great attention had been paid to drill and that all concerned had profited by it. Their drill that day in both close and extended formation had been very good. In conclusion, he said he would have much pleasure in making a favourable report to the authorities and congratulated Colonel Wood upon the honour he had of commanding such a very good battalion.</p>
        <p>The officers present included Lieut.-Colonel Wood (in command) ; Majors W. H. Pride and A. J. Watts; Cap- tains Anderson, Hartley, (honorary major), J. Crean, A. C. Wood, J. G. Whitehead and C. H. Whitney; Lieu- tenants S. H. Perry, T. W. Bruce, L. Watts, J. E. Lloyd, and W. Dickson; Quartermaster J. Dixon; Surgeon- Captains J. W. Ellis and E. W. Barnes; acting chaplain, T. B. Hardern, L.L.D. ; Lieutenant Baillie-Lawson, Tower Hamlets (attached) and Major and Adjutant A. T. Morse. The parade state was 20 officers, 45 sergeants, 48 band and buglers, 1? cyclists, 19 ambulance, 8 signal- lers, 8 pioneers, and 478 rank and file, a total of 613 of all ranks. There were ab-:.ent with leave, 3 officers, 2 sergeants, 2 corporals,and 45 rank and file, and the total enrolled strength of the battalion is 715. The ambulance was in charge of Surgeons-Captain Ellis and Barnes and Hospitil-Sergeant Garvey. Lieutenant Luther Watts was in command of the cyclists; Sergeant Aitkin of the signallers, and Sergeant Hartopp of the pioneer,?, Trooper T. A. Blake, (Denbighsbire Hussars) officiated as Orderly to Colonel Eyre-Williams and the ground was kept by a detachment of the 17th Lancers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DOMESTIC INFELICITY AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>DOMESTIC INFELICITY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A "SCENE" IN THE POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday morn- ing, before Messrs. H. Reynolds, J. Handley, and S. Taylor, Daniel Ankers was summoned by his wife, Mary, for having deserted and refused to maintain her.—Mr. J. Burton, solicitor, of Runcorn, appeared for the wife, and the defendant was unrepresented.—ln his opening statement Mr. Burton add the parties were I married in 1889. The defendant was by trade a boot- maker,and the last house in which he and his wife lived together in Runcorn was 15, Cooper-st. On the 19th May last their youngest child died, and on the 24th the complainant went to her parents' home, which was near Cheater, for the purpose of buryie:g it, expecting that her husband would follow her. Instead of doing so however, as soon as his wife had gone,he sold the furni- ture. and brnkeuu the home and went away. On the 25th May complainant met Lim in Chester and asked him to go home with her. He replied that all was over between thein,and that he would go where he liked and she could go where she liked. He refused to live with her again. She returned to Runcorn and found that the house had been broken up. Consequently she was forced to return to her mother's house to live.— Mary Ankers, wife of the defendant after confirming her solicitor's statement added tnat during the interview with her husband in Chester on the 25th May he threttenen to take her life if she continued to follew him. —Questioned by her husband she denied having cold all the furniture. Neither was he slighted at her mother's house.—Defendant, in his statement to the bench alleged that his wife had sold almost all the furniture before going away,end had broken the home up herself. She had contracted debts, with the baker, grocer, coal- dealer, Sze., and had left his home of her own accord.— The magistrates made an order for the payment of 10s. per week for his wife and child's maintenance, and the cost of the proceedings.—Defendant here interrupted in an excited manner by saying that he would not pay a penny, but would go to gaol. Being requested to leave the Court he refused and upon P.C. Williams attempting to eject him he struggled and kicked very violently striking at the constable with his fists several times. Eventually upon the order of the Superintendent he was removed, with the assistance of other officers, to the cells below and the business of the Sessions proceeded with.— Subsequently he was placed in the dock on a charge of 'assaulting P.C. Williams whikt in the execution of his duty that morning in Court.—Defendant pleaded guilty, and said if the magistrates would impose a fine he would pay it in a short time. Having heard the evidence of the assaulted constable, the bench inflicted a sentence of seven days' imprisonment with hard labour without the option of a fine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>NEWTON BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Newton Commis- sioners was held in the Earlestown Town Hall on Wednesday afternoon. The Commissioners present were—Messrs. J. W.Emmett (chairman), G.Ratcliffe, T. Ormand, &amp;T. Evans,?. MacMahon, G. E. Worsley (law clerk) and R. Brierley (clerk and surveyor). There was also a small number of ratepayers present. Mr. Worsley read the notice convening the meeting, when Mr.Speakman,a ratepayer,rose and asked whether a more convenient time than four o'clock could not be chosen for the meeting. As they were all aware, there was a large number of ratepayers who could not pos- sibly attend at that time, and they were practically ex- cluded frron the meeting. He moved the adjournment of the meeting until 7.30 that evening. Mr.Ernmett in reply said that the meeting had always been held at that time, and the Commissioners ha I only dene the same as they had always done before. If how- ever the matter were brought before the notice of t commissioners next year, he had no doubt that they would have the meeting at a more convenient time.</p>
        <p>the i</p>
        <p>Mr. Speakman said -that he was glad to receive that assurance, and he hoped the Clez k would make a note of it.</p>
        <p>The annual statement of accounts was then submitted to the meeting. Mr. Simpson obj-cted to them being passed at once, as there was not sufficient time for the expenditure to be examined. He was,however,informed that he could have had the particulars any time during the last 15 days, and it was, therefore, his own fault that he had not examined the accounts. A number of qnestions were asked by Mr. Adams relating to various items of expenditure, and were satisfactorily answered by the Chairman. The following is a summary state- ment of the loans of the Commissioners :—Gas works, amount borrowed, £11,350, amount repaid, £8,657 16s. 10d., amount now owing, £2,632 3s. 2d. ; public sewers, £11.750, £9,808, 6s. 81.. £1,941 13s. 4d.; water £3,000, £1.766 13s. 4d., 1.233 6s. Sd. ; public streets. £3,620, 3,270 13s. 41., £349 6s. 81. ; private streets, £11.220 10,.9d. £9,350 125..£1,869 183. 9d. ; cemetery, £3,500, £569 14. Bd., £2,930 ss. 4d. ; public hall and cilices, amount borrowed. £lO,OOO, amount now owing, £lO,OOO total, £54,440 103. 9d., £33,423 16s. 10d., £21,016 13s. lid. The accounts were unanimously passed, on the motion of Mr. MacMabon, seconded by Mr. Speakman. Tne auditors, Messrs. T. Thompson, and J. Gooding were re-elected for the ensuing sear, on the motion of Mr. Simpson, seconded by Mr. Adams. This concluded the business of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
