      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <p>NEWTON, HAYDOCK, AND COLBORNE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BUILDING SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>BUILDING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The alth annual general meeting of the members of this society was held on Wednesday evening at the Town Hall, Newton. Mr. W. H. Arnott, one of the trustees, presided and he was supported by Mr, J. Sankey, Mr. W. Appleby (auditor), and Mr. J. Adams (secretary). There was only a very poor attend- ance of members. The Chairman in openieg the meet- irg called upon the Secretary to read the financial and auditors reports. The Secretary's (Mr. John Adams) report was as under :—" The directors have pleasure in reporting to the members the Society's transactions for the past year, and presenting to them the annexed statement of accounts, certified by the auditors. Advances have been made this year on fourteen lots of property amounting to £5,305, and after deducting repayments the total sum outstanding on properties not in hand is £13,749 03. 9d. on thirty-seven lots, and the interest received from them is £630 7s. 9d. The prafita for the year are £7BO 3s. 6d., and a dividend of five pounds per cent, has been declared, which will absorb £776 ss. lid., leaving £3 17s. 7d. to be carried to the contingent account, which now stands at £2,305 Is. 2d. This is over 90 per cent, on the amount outstanding on properties in the Society's hands, and over 14 per cent. on the total sum outstanding on mortgage. The direc- tors congratulate the members on the sound financial position attained by the Society, and confidently recom- mend their attention to the figures in the balance shect, which show it to be a thoroughly reliable and profitable investment, a dividend of 5 per ceht. having been earned during the past year, and there now being a reserve fund of over £2,300; while they anticipate that the only two properties nmw in hand can be sold with little loss, if any, as they are good properties, and paying a net return of over 5 per cent. The Society consists of 296 members, holding 1,595 invested shares, and 2,0231.- mortgage shares." Mr. W. Appleby, the auditor, in his report, said :—" It affords me much pleasure to note the vastly improved condition of your society, and to draw attention to the fact that there are now only two properties in the hands of the society, and these do not represent more than £2,559 10s. whereas you have a contingent account for ' £2,301 3s. 7d. Further,l am glad to observe the special care which appears to be exercised by your directors in making advances, and trust that the past experiences will be of continual advantage to the society, and there- _ by guarantee to the members a safe security for their deposits. .</p>
        <p>;The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the report, said as one of the trustees they had given him no work to do, and they were evidently trying not to give him anything to do in the coming year. It gave him the greatest pleasure to receive such a favourable report of the society, especially when it was considered what was the general feeling two or three years ago. With the management they had last year he saw no reason why they should not have another successful year. (Ap- plause.) _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Sankey in seconding, said he did not think that he need desctibe anythin, as he had a hand in making the report,and it was no use going over it agaiu. It gave him especial pleasure in presenting the report, because it was the first time in his Rnoxviellge that the society had paid 5 per cent., and that percentage had been honestly earned. (Applause.) The report was then unAnimously passed. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>In the election of officer, Mr. J. S tnkey was unani- mously reappointed Chairman of Directors, and Messo. Liptrott, Wilkinson, and Singleton were re-elected on the Board.—Messrs. W. Appleby and I. Bateman were re-appointed auditors. In expressing his thanks for his re-election, Mr. Appleby said he would take that oppor- tunity of supplementing the report he had already made and which they had in their possession. He had ex- pressed already by his printed report, the confidence ho had in the society, and also the expression with regard to his opinion as to its stability. There were not many societies like theirs, not many that could boast of being in a sound financial position, in England. His firm had had a very large experience with Buildigg Societies, and it afforded him very great pleasure to give that testi- mony. There had been some societies which had become notorious on account of the manner in which they had been conducted in the past. That society had been un- fortunate and met losiies,but their experience in the past had stood them well during the past five years. Allud- ing to the introduction of the Bill into the House of Com- mons with regard to the publication of Building Society accounts, he said he did not think it would touch them' as they already fulfilled the principles of the Act. He again thanked them for re-appointing him auditor. (Ap- plause.) Mr. H. J. Longton, solicitor, said from a legal point of view there was nothing for him to say. He had at- tended the meeting officially with the object of answer- ing any question which any shareholder had to ask him but in the absence of any such questions he did not know' that he had anything to say in regard to the affairs cf till society. He thought the prospects of the society, were better to day that he had ever known them, and he hoped it would go on prospering. (Appause.) On the motion of Mr. J. Sankey, seconded by Mr. A. Pimblett, a vote of thanks was accorded the chairman and the meeting terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>A. LOCIL FRIENDLY SOCIETY'S ACTION.</head>
        <p>A. LOCIL FRIENDLY SOCIETY'S ACTION.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before is Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, Mr. Langdon, barrister, instructed by Messrs. Cobbett, Wheeler and Cobbett, solicitors, 3lanchester,applied for an order of fore-closure with respect to property at Earlestown. Mr. Graham, solicitor, Wigan, appeared on behalf of the defendant, John Butterfield.—Mr. Lang- don stated that the plaintiffs were Thomas Tunstall, David Williams and Robert Whitfield, trustees of the Ashton-in-Makerfield district of the Independent Order of Oddfellows Manchester Unity Friendly Society, and the defendants were John Butterfield, and his son John James Tatham Butterfield. On the 29th November, 1893, the lodge agreed to lend to John Butterfield, of Wallgate, Wigan, builder and contractor, the sum of £BO upon the security of a plot of land at the corner of Athol-street and Portland-street in the township of Earlestown containing 556 square yards. By an agreement in writing dated the 2nd December. 1893, the defendant, John James Tatham Butterfield. who was a son of the defendant Jehn Butterfield, agreed to deposit with the plaintiffs, Thomas Tunstall, arrd David Williams (the then trustees of the lodge) and on its behalf, a lease dated, the 30th November, 1893,mado- between Richard Evans and Co., Ltd., and the last named defendant, whereby the land was demised to. him for the residue of a term of 999 years from the 25th March, 1887, securing payment to the plaintiffs of £BO, with interest at 5 per cent. The £BO having been paid' over to the defendant John Butterfield, the latter, de- posited the lease with them. The younger defendant never had any interest in the land,or the lease, and was a trustee for his father, entering into the agreement at his request and by his direction. The £BO was still owing, and no interest had been paid. The interest now due amounted to £1 12s. Bd. The plaintilfsi asked that his Honour would give them a declara- tion that the younger defendant was a trustee of the land, and of the lease for: his father. They also desired that the land,</p>
        <p>should be vested in the defendant J-o-h-n-, Butterfield. or the plaintiffs, or such other person as the. court might direct.—Mr. David Williams bore out Mr. Langdon's statement, and also remarked that John But-. terfield told him that his son accompanied him to the solicitors. at the time of the transaction,lor the purpose of obtaining an insight into the business. Mr. Vere B. Davies, of Messrs. Davies, Kirkconnel, and Burgess, also gave evidence respecting the agreement.—George Gough a member of the lodge committee, said he went to the defendant, John Butterfield, and charged him with ob- taining money by false pretences. Defendant replied "I admit like a man that you are deceived between me and my son." Asked why he introduced his son in the business he said he did it for his own purpose. The son, was present at the interview and did not deny what his father had said. His Honour stated that from what had been disclosed by the last witness be thought it was a questionable proceeding. Mr. Langdon contended that the plaintiffs were innocent parties, and he asked how could the aetion of any other party be a bar to their• obtaining their legal rights ?—His Honour pointed out that the present proceedings might be set aside. Other creditors besides the plaintiffs would he affected, and they would have a prior right.—Mr. Langdon said he. thought that would not be the case. Here the father got their money upon the undertaking to give a legal mortgage. They, however, found that the mortgage which was going to be given by the father was given by the son. The son had been the subject of a fraud which the father had contrived for his own pur- poses.—His Honour said what did that mean but that the man intended to deceive his creditors. Other cr,di- tors would be entitled to come in before the present. plaintiffs. He was being asked to declare a fraud.— Mr.Langdon contended that that was not so,but that he• was asked to declare that the younger detendant was the trustee of the land.—His Honour made the declarer tion.—The hearing of the second part of the application was deferred to the next court day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CHRISTIAN POLICE ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN POLICE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of the members and friends of the Christian Police Association was held in the Y.M.C.A. rooms on Tuesday afternoon. Miss fiesie- Dutton presided, and Miss Jones acted as organist. Miss Dutton in opening the meeting expressed her. pleasure at seeing so many present. She hoped that the association would grow in number, arid that very soon a large proportion of the police of the town would join with them. She knew that t.here. were many temptations to contend with, but she hoped that they would stand firm and not give way to any of them. The !n.-.e*in4 t for anyone to give an nddress, and alter a very en- joyaWe service, the meeting was closed by thoi;.ro- nouncing of the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
