      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>STUDENT'S SONG.</head>
        <p>THE STUDENT'S SONG.</p>
        <p>I hcl worke4 hard at Euclid. and Latin, and Greek, Till my eyesight was dim, and my chest was quite weak ; There were pains in my head, there were pains in my back. And I felt just as if I'd been placed on a rack. They sent for a Doctor, they sent tor a Nurse. But all was in vain, I grew rapidly worse, Spite of poultice and potion, and powder and pill. Wl,,en—who should come in but my old friend, dearlWill. Kr-cried," Cheer up, old fellow, we'll soon have you well, As strong as an ox. and as sound as a bell; All you want's Quinine Bitters—of med'cine the best, And cricket and football will go all the test." I took Quinine Bitters and found it was true. Now with weakness and pain I have tv4thing to do; And as for the potions, and powders, and pills, Why, the Doctor may take them himself if he wills. Now'. "Hurrah for the Bitters," of tonics the King, shout till the roof and rafters shall ring ; And I'll say to whoever may enter my door, •• It's the best of all med'cines for rich or for poor."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>G WILY M EVANS</head>
        <p>G WILY M EVANS</p>
