      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>" PEGGY "PLAYING IN THE STREETS. Arthur Ashcroft, 26 Dannett-st., Louis Bibby, 15 Eldon-st., Thomas Howard, 7 Eldon-st., were each charged with playing " tipcat " on Sunday June 10th, the former in Hamilton-st., and the latter two in Eldon- ot. It was explained that" tipcat "was the game com- monly known as " peggy." The Ctief Constable said there were numerous complaints about this game being played. Large numbers of windows had been broken in consequence,and the police tild determined to put a stop to it.—The defendants were fined 25. 6d. each. THE WANT OF A PUBLIC RECREATION GROUND. A number of youths named James Booth, James Frith Albert Machin, Robert Donaldson, Andrew Donaldson, Edward Donald, Wm. Fox, John Bellis. James Lomax, Charles Picktn, and Henry Greenhalgh, were each charged at the Warrington Borough Court on Monday, with playing " rounders " in Hamilton-st., on June 10th to the annoyance of passengers. P.C. Turner proved the case. Mr. Wm. Pierpoint said that he thought places of recreation ought to be provided in various parts of the town, aud the C)rporation should provide such places. —The magistrates dismissed defendants with a caution THEFT OF RINGS.</p>
        <p>A girl, 15 years of age, named Mary Elizabeth Shep- herd, pleaded guilty at Warrington County Petty Ses- sions on Wednesday, to stealing on the 7th June. two gold rings value 255. the property of Wm. Lewis Rigby, Earlestown. Mrs. RiL-,,by stated that she lived with her husband at 82 Regent-st., Earlestown. she had been ill for some time,and had engaged the prisoner to assist her in the housework. On the 17th of the present month wit- ness changed her dress, and gave it to the prisoner to hang up. The two gold rings produced were in the pocket of the dress at the time, and she valued them at 2.5 a. Witness went out. On her return she went for the rings she had left in her pocket, but found that they were gone. The prisoner denied touching the rings. The following day the police were informed. Constable Twaddle said that he in company with Sergeant Long- worth went to the house of the complainant. The prisoner was there, and she denied that she had taken the rings. Witness told her that the house would be searched, whereupon she put her hand up a bedroom chimney, and pulled out the two rings produced. She was charged with the theft and replied " Yes I took them." *As this was her first offonce, the magistrates bound her over to appear when called upon. nth GRIEF CONSTABLE ON GAMBLING. Patrick Murray, 3 Berry's-yard, was charged at the Warrington Borough Court, on Monday, with playing pitch and toss on Sunday morning in Silver-square. P.C. Evans proved the case. The Chief Constable said that this case was not a very strong case, and he always strove to get the police to err on the side of mercy than the other Sway, and he did not wish them to lock up any one of previous good character. They would, however, have to be more strict with the habit of playing pitch and toss than they had hitherto been. During Saturday afternoon, Sunday, and Monday morning, there was a number who were at this the whole of the time. He said it with all respect but he thought that the magistrates had been sauly too lenient in cases of this sort which had been brought by the police before them. In one or two cases, where the law had been rigourously enforced, the practice had been checked for a week or two. It was a great difficulty for the police to get the names of the people indulging in this habit as they clubbed together and would not give any names if possible.—The Bench inflicted a fine of 23.6d.in this case,and also upon Charles Nicholls, 16 Jones Buildings, for committing the same offence. They hoped that everybody would bear in mind that a fine of 40s. and costs,,tr a month's imprison- ment could be inflicted for such offences.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>MINOR CASES. -- WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. -- WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, and Mr. S. Wright.</p>
        <p>DR/NlL—Alice Green, no address, an elderly woman, was discharged on account of her age, on a charge of being drunk in Church-st. the previous afternoon. CHARGE OF DEFRAUDING A TRADESMAN.—Mary Stone, a married woman living in Bostock-st.,was charged with stealing by means of a trick on the 2nd June and divers other days,a certain quantity of provisions,value 17s. lid. the property of Mr. Wm. Allen, provision dealer, Mill- lane. The complainant stated that on the date named, the defendant came into his shop and asked that she might become a weekly customer. She told him that her husband was the bailiff at the County Court, War- rington, and that he (witness) knew him. Witness told the defendant that she must pay 'what was owing at the week-end, or she would not be able to have any further credit. During the week. the defendant made several statements as to her husband taking possession on behalf of the County Court at different places out of town. On the following Saturday (June 9) she told him the same tale, and brought no money in payment of the goods she had had during the week. She said that she would bring the money on the following Monday morn- ing when her husband- came home. As she did not turn up, witness made inquiry at the County Court, and found that the defendant's husband had not been there for some weeks previously. After some further evi- dence, the case was adjourned for three weeks. The magistrates advised the defendant to make some arrange- ment with the complainant for the payment or the debt.</p>
        <p>ASSAULTING A LODGER.—Jane Dig2le, Turner-st., was charged with having on the 2nd June assaulted Patrick Gorden, Percival's-fold. Mr. A. Browne appeared for the complainant. de stated that the prosecutor was a labourer at the Warrington wire ropery and had lodged with the defendant. On the date named some alterca- tion took place between the complainant and another lodger about a pipe. On the complainant going out the defendant struck him on the head with a jug, which caused a great loss of blood. The complainant corro- borated Mr. Browne's statement, and said he had been unable to follow his employment regularly in conse- quence of the injury —A fine of 105. and costs was imposed. CHARGE OF ASSAULT.—Mrs. Ireland, Hill-st., was dis- missed on a charge of assaultinc, Eliza Hill,Dakin's-yard, on the 9th June. The complainant's statement was unsupported, and the defendant denied toe charge. SATURDAT.—Before Mr. J Burgess (chairman) and Mr</p>
        <p>S. Chandley. ABSENTEE FRPM THE MILITIA.—WiIIiaM3 Hughes was remanded to await escort on the charge of absenting himself from the An.:•lesea Militia. TuE SHARPEST PICKPOCKET TN WARRINGTON.—TheTSR Gl,•ave, a girl of 14 years, was charged with stealing from the person of Margaret Woods,in the Market Place, on Wednesday last, a purse containing 33.10 d. The</p>
        <p>SOVEREIGN, REMEDIES FOR SKIN ERUPTIONS. To Cure Eczema, Skin 02E1E113 lirottions, Irritation, Pim- ples, Blotches, Ulcerated Bad Legs and Sores, use CULLWICK'S SKIN OINTMENT AND BLOOD TABLETS. WORLD-WIDE REPUTATION. Either Ointment or 'I ablets, is. I id., from all Chemists ; or post free Is. 3d.. from MARTIN. Chemist, Southampton. BOOTS, Cash Chemists, Warrington. Agents { SPEAKM AN, 7, Bridge-st., Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>prisoner had been brought before the magistrates on Thursday, in company with two other females charged with suspicious conduct, and the prisoner had been re- manded until Saturday owing to the fact of a purse being found on her. On Friday morning Mr. Woods, the com- plainant having noticed a paragraph in the newspaper as to the purse being found, having lost her own in the market on Wednesday, went to the policestation, snd it was then found that the description of the purse was similar to the purse found in the prisoner's possession. The prisoner was accordingly charged with the thef t.— The Chief Constable said that tho prisoner was the sharpest pickpocket in Warrington, and was always about when there was a crowd.—The father said that he had no control over the girl whatever, and he wished the bench to send her away to some h )me. After a long discussion, the bench decided to send the prisoner for one month, and if a place could be found for her, they ordered that she be sent thither for five years. MONDAY.—Beforo Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Alderman Francombailderman Wright, Mr. W. Pierpoint, and Mr. S. Wright. DRUNKENNESS.—The following were dealt with for this offence : Thomas Mitchell, 16 Hill-street, fined ss. and costs ; Edward Sauce, 15 James-street, 5.; Peter Moran, Burtonwood, 2s. 6d. ; Charles Harrop, B Egerton-street, ss. and costs. ONLY A SIXPENNY TABLE KNlFE.—Frank Burke, Friars Green, was charged with stealing from the Lion Hotel on Saturday morning, a table knife value 61., the property of Peter Walker and Sons. The evidence showed that the prisoner came into the Lion Hotel on Saturday morning and asked for some bread and cheese. This was supplied him and also the knife. In going out it,was noticed that the prisoner had put the knife up his sleeve, and was taking it away. The prisoner was then followed, and was seen to go into another public heuse. P.C. Forster was informed, and the prisoner was taken to the police station—The prisoner denied that he had stolen the knife saying that he would not steal such a small thing as a sixpenny table knife. He was how- ever unable to explain how it got up his sleeve, and was therefore sent to gaol for 14 days. HURTING HIS DIGNITY.—DanieI Crosby, 15 Hill-street, was charged with stealing by means of a trick on Satur- day evening, is. 10d. in cash, the property af Joseph Pilling. The Chief Constable stated tho prisoner did odd jobs for people in the market, and on Saturday, he was sent to water Filling's horse. When he returned,he said that the horse had no food, and wished to go for some for it. Pilling however said that he would send someone else. This appeared to hart the prisoner's dignity, for he wanted to know whether Pilling thought he wa3 a thief. Pilling in the end cave him 2s. 41. to get some food for the horse from Mr. Barlow,flour dealer VVinwick-street. The prisoner however, only bought ed. worth of food, and upon being asked for the change re- fused to give it up. Information was given to the police and the prisoner was taken to the police station.—The prisoner said that he had intended to have given Pilling the change back again later on, but he was annoyed at Piling not trusting him. Evidence was given in sup- port of the charge by Frank Wilson, the complainant and Detective Monaghan, and the prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 days. A DANGEROUS Doe.—Brian Barry, 40 Lord Nelson- street, was charged with keeping a dog unmuzzled on June 16th. A great number of complaints were brought against the dog, and it was stated that it had bitten several boys. The case was adjourned for 14 days in order that the defendant might destroy the dog. OBSTRUCTING THE STREET.—John Connaugton, Lyrnm, was fined ss. for leaving his cart too long in Market- street on June 15th. Betsy Rutter, Hattori, was fined 2s. 61. for the same offence in Bridge-street. CHIMNEY ON FlRE.—David Johnson, 11 Beatrice-street was fined a shilling for having his chimney on fire on June 3rd.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Befora Mr; R. Garnett (chairman), Dr. Starkie Smith, and Mr. E J. Guest. ASSAULTING THE POLlCE.—Edward Welsh was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Marshhouse-lane the previous afternoon, and further charged with assaulting P.C. Sankey whilst in the execution of his duty. Pri- soner pleaded guilty.—P.C. Sankey stated that he was on duty at Orford Barracks, the previous afternoon, when the recruits for the South Lancashire Militia were assembling. The defendant was there, and had been turned out of the barracks-yard by Sergeant String- fellow, on account of being drunk. Witness asked him to go away several times, but he refused, and he was obliged to lock him up. In doing so, the defendant tup- ped him in the chest with his head. An officer from the barracks corroborated this statement. Prisoner was fined 103. and cost a or 14 days' imprisonment. ANOTHER SHOP DOOR THEFT.—Thomas Quinn, was charged with stealing from the shop door of Mr. Arthur Booth, Butterm arket-st., a pair of men's laced-up boots, value 7s. 11d. The Chief Constable stated that the pri- soner came from Manchester on Friday. The previous afternoon, P.C. Clough was standing at the end of Horsemarket-st. when he saw the prisoner, with a pair of boots under his arm, inquiring the way to St. Helens. Constable Clough asked the prisoner for an explanation about the toots. His answers not being satisfactory, the constable took him to the shop where he said he had bought the boots. It was found that the boots had not been sold, but had been taken from one of the hocks at the show entrance. A ticket with the price of the boots marked on, was found upon the prisoner. On being charged with the offence his reply was "My brother John told me to go and price a pair of hoots and I took them." The man was under the influence of drink at the time. Mr. Booth on being questioned by the magis- trates said the boots were hanging about a yard from the doorway. The prisoner was committed to gaol for one month hard labour. SLEEPING OUT.—Ann Moran, a stranger in the town. was charged with sleeping in a yard of an untenanted house in Church-st., without any visible means of sub- sistence. She was discharged on promising to leave the town at once. SCHOOL CASES.—Mr. G. Riley prosecuted in the follow- ing cases of parents summoned for irregular attendance of their children at school : Henry Sherwood, 11, Dial- at,, hawker, 5.: Joseph Clough, file cutter, dismissed; John Edwards, 38, Coclezedge-lane, labourer, ss. ; Joseph Hill, 31, Watkin-st., wire drawer, 2s. 6d.; J. Knight. Silver-square, labourer, 5 ; Charles Parry. 42, Eliza- beth-st., fireman, 2s. 6d.; Thomas Shovelton, 32, Wat- kin-st., hawker. 2s. 6d. ; Robert Taylor, 10, Hart-st., dismissed; William Tryner, 46, Pierpoint-st., forge- man, 53.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. J. Fa'rclough (e1)-iirman, J. Geddes, and J. IL Fleming. _ _</p>
        <p>FOWL STEALING.—SamueI Lee, a married' man residing at No. 1, Watkin-st.,was charged with stealing between the hours of 8 p.m. on Monday night and 5 a.m.on Tues- day morning, a cock and two hens and a drake and two ducks, value 18s., from the hen coop of Wm. Mills, 2, George-at. The complainant, who is a carpenter in the London and North-Western Railway sheds, stated that a lad named John Johnson,who lives with him, had locked the hen coop an seen that the fowls were all there at 8 o'clock on Monday night. At 5 a.m.on Tuesday,Johnson discovered that the hen coop had been forced open, and the six fowls abstracted. He called the complainant, Mills, and the two on looking about discovered tracks of a pair of clogs leading from the yard, across a field, to the railway embankment. Constable Chrimes said that he was on duty in Bewsey-road at about half-past six when he was told by Mills of the fowls being stolen the previous night. He went to the house aod with the lad Johnson followed the tracks across the fields to the rail way embankment adjoining the Horse and Jockoy Inn on Winwick-road. Near the embankment they noticed two heaps of soil, and on investigating them discovered three of the fowls beneath each. The officer took the fowls back to Mr. Mills, who identified them as his pro- perty. The complainant stated that the prisoner had done some work for him some time before, and that he thought a dos( which was in the yard when the theft was committed would have given the alarm had anyone but the prisoner entered. Sergeant Smith not *find- ing Chrimes on his beat proceeded to the defendant's house. After acquainting the sergeant with the faets of the theft, Chrimes accompanied him to the prisoner's address (21,Watkin-st.) but found that he had left. On going away they met Lee just coming out of Ni. 1, in the same street,and perceiving that his clothes were wet and had feathers and marks of blood upon them,hrought him to the Police Station. Detective-Inspector Monag- han took the clogs from the prisoner and found that they tallied with the tracks in every particular. On being asked if he had anything to say about the fowls, Lee replied, "I know nowt about 'em."—As this was his second offence, the magistrates sentenced him to three months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. Si EDNESDAL—Before Major Pickmere (chairman), and Councillor James Tinsley.</p>
        <p>CHARGES OF USING THREATS.—Jetnima Bunt sum- moned her husband for using threats to her at Sankey Bridges. The defendant did n:,t, appear. P.C. Ihigdale said he served the summons on the defendant, but the house was now empty, and he did not know where he had gone to —The complainant said her husband had sold all the furnitute, and she believed he had gone to Cornwall. The magistrates advised her to apply to the parish for relief, and the authorities would take steps to find her hushand.—Mikelan. tanners' labourer, Orford, was charged with using threats to John Ford, at Orford, on the 17th June. The complainant said the defendant came to his lodgings drunk on Sunday, at two o'clock in the morning, and told him that " he would run a knife through him." Witness, however, was not afraid of the man doing him any harm. The case was dismissed.</p>
        <p>ALLOWING COWS TO STRAY.—James GIP3V(., farmer, Croft. was charged with having on the 4th June at Croft allowed two cows to stray on the highway. P.C. Pope proved the ease. The defendant was fined 2s. 6d. and costs. DOG AT LARGE.—Thomas Green, Warrington, was fined Is. and costs for allowing a aog to be at large on the sth June, at Houghton Greeß.</p>
        <p>Fu SEALSKIN JACKETS AND FURS of every description CLEANED or RE-DYED and ALTERED. FUR SKINS dressed and made up into rugs, &amp;c.—B CooKF.. Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper Newing- ton, lnd 21, Renshaw-street, Liverpool. Established 20 ea.-3.</p>
      </div>
