      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>Handley and S.-Taylor.- IRREGULAR ATTENDANCE AT SCHOOL.—Joseph Basnett, of Stone-street was fined 6th and 4s. costs for not send- ing his child regularly to school. AN ALLEGED UNREGISTERED LODGING-HOUSE—Michael Phillips, who did not put in an appearance, was charged with having kept a house in Mersey-street, open for the reception of nightly lodgers, such house not being regis- tered as a common lodging-house as required by the Public Health Act.—Sergeant Lee spoke to visiting the dwelling and to finding four lodgers therein.—Upon the application of Superintendent Atherton a warrant was granted for the apprehension of the defendant Phillips. AN UNISUZZLED Doo.—For allowing his dog to stray in Halton-road on the morning of the 3rd inst., minus the necessary muzzle, John Davies was tined 6d. and costs. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY AND ASSAULTING THE POLICE. —Thomas Woodtine was charged with being drunk and disorderly in King-street on the night of the 16th inst., and also with assaulting P.C. John Richardson whilst in the execution of his duty on that occasion.—Defendant pleaded guilty to both charges.—P.C. Gregory testified to seeing the defendant kick the constable twice on the leg whilst the latter was attempting to rcmove him to the police station for being drunk and disorderly.—Defend- ant, who had previously been convicted for a similar offence, was fined ss. aid costs in eacli case or an alter- native of fourteen days imprisonment. ASSISTING IN THE KEEPING OF A BROTHEL.—Catherine lowden and Matilda Holland, both women of ill-repute were sentenced to one month's imprisonment with hard labour for aiding and abetting Ellen Moran in keeping a disorderly house in Mersey-street, Runcorn, on the sth June.—The charge was proved by Sergt. Lee.</p>
        <p>WIDVS POLICE COURT. MONDAT.—Before Councillor W. W. Gossage. DRUNIIENNESS.—The following persons were fined for being drunk and disorderly :—Thomas Cooney,Flutchin- son-st.; Patrick Finn, Ditton ; James Murphy, Eleanor- a'.; James Rock, Cromwell-st. ; Arthur Ratcliffe, Hurst- st., and Sarah Ann Leigh and Michael Conolly, of no ited abode. BEGGING.—For begging invictoria-road, on Saturday, John McCafferty, of Lugsdale-road, was sentenced to seven days' imprisonment. ALLEGED THEFT OF A WATCH.—Michael Carr, no fixed abode, was brought up on remand charged with having stolen a silver watch. It appears that he was seen loiter- ing near West Bank Dock about 2 a.m. on Thursday morning and was arrested on suspicion. As sufficient evidence was not forthcoming, he was discharged.</p>
        <p>LYMM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Mr. T. P. Norris (chairman), Mr. C.</p>
        <p>Lister and Dr. Brabaion.</p>
        <p>Don OWNERS AGAIN !—For allowing their dogs to be at large without muzzles, the following were dealt with: Thos. Matts, on the 21st April at Lymm, 3s. 6d.; Myra Heaton, on the Bth June at Lymm, 35. 6d.; and George Miller on the 25th ult. at Statham, 3s. 6d. DRUNK.—Wm. Atkinson, Lymm, was fined 10s. and costs for being drunk at 1.40 a.m. on the 26th ult., at Higher Lane, Lymm. The defendant had been sum- moned at three successive Sessions for similar offences. The Chairman remarked that if the defendant appeared again before them he would be sent to prison without the option of a line. FURIOUS DRlVlNG.—Charles Owen, butcher, in the employ of Mr. Winstanley, Lymm, was charged with having on the sth inst. at Partington driven furiously so as to endanger the lives of pedestrians.—P.C. Bebbing- ton stated that he saw the defendant driving past his station at Partington at a furious rate, and was thrash- ing it at the same time. Witness stopped him on his return, and the horse seem frightened. The defendant was driving at the rate of 14 or 15 miles an hour. He was fined 7s. 6d. including costs. BICYCLE ON THE FOOTPATH.—Joseph Hugh, Ditchfield lane, High Legli, was dismissed on a charge of riding a bicycle on the footpath. Mr. Lister, one of the magis- trates,said the road was a by-lane, and was in very bad condition, and no person could ride a bicycle upon it. The defendant said he and his brother kept the footpath in order. CHARGE OF ASSAULT.—James Brookes, farmer, Thel- wall, was charged with assaulting a farm labwurer,named Lawrence Oakes, at Thelwall, on the 12th inst. The complainant stated that he was driving some cows when one of them rambled into the defendant's field. The defendant came to witness complaining, saying it was due to his carelessness. Witness denied it, where- upon the defendant struck him in the stomach, knock- ing him to the ground. Michael Mannion,said he saw the defendant getting up from the ground, but did not see how he got down. (Laughter.) The case was dis- missed. THE CRUELTY TO A PONY.—H. Smalley, boatman, Run. was again charged with having on the sth May crelly illtreating a pony by working it in an unfit state. The case was remanded from the previous sessions, in order to ascertain whether the defendant's statement, that the animal was destroyed, was true. Inspector Luff, R.S.P.C.A.. said that the animal had been destroyed. The defendant was ordered to pay the costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>SHOCKING SUICIDE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A filecutter, named William Ball, 48 years of age, of 22, Manchester-road, pointed a pistol at his right temple, and shot himself dead,on Tuesday morning, Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy county coroner held an in- quest in the Parade Room, on Wednesday afternoon touching the death of the deceased. Mary Bali was the first witness called, and said the deceased was her husband. He had been drinking heavily since lie had the influenza at Christmas. Dr. McCarthy had been attending him, for the last three months for a weak heart. The deceased became very low spirited, mad threatened to take his own life, and witness's also. She became afraid of him, and left him the ,previous Satur- day. He had often abused her. Latterly, whenever he took any drink, he became very strange in his talk and manner. She did not see the deceased alive again after she left him. The reason she did not inform the polic of the deceased's conduct was that she thought that it was a pity to get him sent to prison when he couldn't help it. It was only when he was in drink that he ill- used her, and he always expressed his sorrow when he was sober. In answer to Inspector Spinks, witness said she had never seen the pistol, produced before. Eliza- beth Ann Ball, daughter of the deceased stated that her father returned to the house about eleven o'clock in the morning. He was strange in his manner. He shook hands with witness, and took the baby upon his knee, 1 and kissed it, saying "God bless you, that is the last time I shall kiss you alive." He said he was going to cut his throat, and "did not know whether to do the baby or not." Witness snatched the baby from II him, and hid the knives in a drawer. She went out to ' I inform the police and took the baby with her. When she had got about twenty yards from the house, she heard the report of a pistol. She met Sergeant Chantler and told him. Witness did not go back to the house for some time.—Wm. Henry Ball, son of the deceased said he went home for his dinner about a quarter past twelve on Tuesday. From what he was told by some neighbours he went upstairs to his father's bedroom and lound him lying on his back in the room. There was a pistol in his right hand which witness took from him. His father was alive, and witness did not know that deceased had injured himself until he saw a swelling on the right tem- ple. Deceased died a few hours afterwards. Mr. Auk- land, relieving officer, said the deceased was ill about Christmas, and received parish relief. He saw him between 11-15 and 11-30 on Tuesday morning. Deceased then seemed quite rational, and when they passed de- ceased said "good morning " to him. Deceased did not seem to have had any drink. He shot himself about ten minutes after seeing witness. About an hour al terwards witness visited the house. The deceased was then not quite dead but was unconscious. Dr. McCarthy attend- ed the deceased but there was no hope whatever of his recovery. He died about 230. Inspector Spinks said two reports were heard, but there was only one mark on the deceased's face.— The Coroner said it was a very sad case indeed. It was quite clear that the deceased took away his own life. The only question was whether the jury thought the deceased was of sound mind when he took away his life, or whether they considered he was of unsound mind at the time. The jury returned a verdict that the deceased committed suicide whilst of unsound mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>• WORSHIPPING THE QUEEN'S STATUE.</head>
        <p>• WORSHIPPING THE QUEEN'S STATUE.</p>
        <p>The Calcutta correspondent of the "Times "sends the following telegram :—Yesterday (Tuesday) the Queen's statue at Madras was smeared with Hind on religious marks on the brehead. neck, and breast. The police in- quiry has resulted in the opinion being expressed that it was the work of a Elitldno who desired to worship the statue. This is not the first time that such a smearinc, has taken place. Some time ago a carpenter was caught in the act of decmating th'b statue with garlands, and marks similar to those now found were detected on that occasion. Ile said that he was worshipping the great MaharanQe, who he hoped would protect him and give him plenty of work. The Inspector of police in whose division the statue is situated says that he himself has noticed people burning incense, breaking cocoanuts,and prostrating themselves in worship before it.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Flrra Annie Steel is Mr. Kipling:9 most formid- able rival in Anglo-Indian fiction. Mrs. Steel is middle- aged ; her hair is quite grey, but she has a fresh- coloured face, which is the very personification of gaid- ness, good sense, and good humour,the last brought out very strongly by the twinkle in her eyes. It is a typical motherly face, made Scotch by the prominent cheek bones.</p>
