        <p>The Angling exhibition !which was held the other week was not so isuccessful:as it deserved, although there are many circumstances which tend to take away the interest of Warringtouians in angling. The river is so polluted with chemicals and filth,that all the fish have been driven out of it for almost a generation. The streams in the district also, notably Sankey brook, have in a greater or lesser degree shared the same fate, and therefore the followers of Izaak Walton have to go a consider- able distance from Warrington, before they can indulge in their favourite pastime. He must there- fore be somewhat of an enthusiast, who indulges in a sport which necessitates so much trouble.</p>
        <p>it is to be hoped that, as Mr. Robert Davies said in his speech at the exhibition, the present genera- tion will see the river pure again, as it once was. This is a possibility according to Lord Playfair,who said, some time ago, that if left to nature, the river would be cleansed in two or three years. This esti- mate by the way,is very different from the estimate which was given at a meeting of the Warrington Rural Sanitary Authority about Si:C. months ago, with respect to the time nece,?..sary for Sankey Brook to become a clear and limpid stream again. It was then stated that it would take 100 years to acc:4m- plish this! Sankey Brook,which it must be remem- bered flows into the Mersey must therefore either be much more contaminated than the Mersey, or one of the estimates must be considerably wide of the mark. There is, however, no sign of anything being done in this direction, and it will therefore be many years before salmon fie hing is once again indulged in at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Rev. John Bond's first visit to Bold Street Chapel last week, was in every way a success. His sermons were of the practical kind, especially that of Sunday morning, and lie will no doubt be welcomed at any future visit he may make to War- rington. Ills lecture on Monday night, on " The firing of the beacons," being on account of the defeat of the Invincible Armada, was an excellent one, and was much enjoyed by those who heard it. The rev, gentleman has made the Elizabethan period of English history his special study, and he was therefore able to speak with authority upon his subject. Mr. Bond is a very prominent Wesley- an minister, and has several times been mentioned as a possible President-, of the Wesleytin Conference. He holds numerous offices in connection with the denomination, so that it is probable that he may at no distant aate, obtain the highest honour which it is possible for a Wesleyan minister to attain.</p>
        <p>"There's nothing like leather," says an old adage, and a Warrington forgeman named William Ellison seems to have thought so too. He, however,wished to become possessed of a sample at the expense of the manager of the Staffordshire House Boot stores, and taking a fancy to a good strong pair of boots he saw hanging near the doorway, he generously helped himself. But another person of conspicuous appearance becoming acquainted with the forgeman's desire for cheapness, accom- modated him still further with food and lodge ings free at his headquarters in Irlam street. The magistrates also last week thought it advisable to maintain him for a month at Her Majesty's expense. The penalty may be thought by some to be well- deserved, but it is doubtful whether the occurrence would have taken place had it not been for the boots having been placed within easy reach.</p>
