        <p>A case of a similar kind was before the magis- trates on Thursday. In Buttermarket street, three girls were able to finger a number of rolls of print, which had been placed on the footpath. The shop- man was not within sight, and the result was that he temptation was too great for the girls. One of hem handed a piece of cloth to her sister, who put it under her shawl, and was going away with it, when they were espied by a policeman. Alderman Sutton, in ordering the girls to be bound over to appear when called upon,took occasion to comment upon this very undesirable custom of shopkeepers in showing their goods, and considered that when it was found necessary to exhibit goods outside, some- one should be left in charge. The suggestion see,ms worthy of consideration.</p>
        <p>Some month's ago it was report edthat a portion of some property in Cambria Place, had, owing to old age and decay, fallen to the ground, and that some people, including the tenants, had a narrow escape from being buried beneath the debris. The houses are, I notice, still standing, without the necessary front portion, and in a most dilapi dated condition. It is remarkable that they have not, before now, been blown down, or collapsed altoge- ther. Children play in and near the empty, and half demolished houses, and yet it seems nobody's business to have them removed. MERLIN.</p>
        <p>THE STRIKES AT WARRINGTON, SETTLENIENTS ARRIVED AT.</p>
        <p>The masons, plumbers, and plasterers, who have been out on strike,v.rere unable to arrive at a settle- ment with the masters upon the points in dispute, until Thursday night, when an amicable settlement was arrived at between the masons and their em- ployers, and a partial settlement was made by the plumb6rB. The masons demanded an increase of a td. per hour, and certain alterations in the work- ing rules. The alterations were agreed to and an offer of an increase of id. per hour was accepted by the men, who resumed work on Friday morning. The principal point still at issue in the plumbing trade dispute is the restriction of the number of apprentices, and it is expected that during next week, an adjustment of the matter will be arrived at. The joiners also put in an application for an increase of id. per hour, coupled with a re-arrange- ment of the working rules. They gave notice of , their intention to strike for these concessions, but { they were met in a fair spirit by the employers and the strike was fortunately avoided. They were , granted an increase of Id. per hour and the amend- ments of the rules were agreed to. A dispute be- tween the master builders and the bricklayers of the town is anticipated next week. The brick- layers of Manchester have been conceded an ad- vance of id. per hour, and as the Warrington branch is affiliated to the Manchester Society, it is expected that they will make a demand for a similar advance.</p>
        <p>WANT OF SCHOOL ACCOMMODATION Al WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>During the week there have been posted on the doors of the various places of worship in the town, notices from the Education Department to the effect that public elementary school accommodation is required for 270 infants near the site of the present Cockhedge Mission school, which will be removed from the list of schools receiving grants on the 30th September next. We understand that the School Attendance Committee have come to the conclusion that the accommodation required can be provided at SiNer-street school and the National schools. It is stated, however, that the Education Department are imperative in their demand for special accommodation and that unless it is provided within a reasonable time steps will be instituted for the formation of a School Board.</p>
