      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>LIFE'S TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>LIFE'S TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>BY MR. H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>The American people have lots of live topics before them just now for discussion, and, if they will only give their time to them a little, they will find some of them tough subjects. Not least among these is the one known as the Coxey army movement. Although Coxey only arrived at Wash- ington with something like 500 followers, this by no means represents the numbers who are set with their faces towards the Capitol of the United States. There are over a dozen armies with an aggregate number of possibly 10 or 15,000 men who claim to be American citizens, and who state that they want work, and, that their families are starving. Coxey, and one or two or the leaders have been arrested and are now out on bail waiting for the verdict. The kind regards of the Government will possibly be extended towards these peaceable breakers of the law in the shape of a fine of £4O and from 60 to 120 days' restful confinement. Some little blood has already been shed and a few lives lost. One of the armies is stranded away in lowa. Camping on one the large rivers they are busy building boats in which they expect to make their way East. The boats are sixteen feet long by six feet wide and draw one foot of water. Each is supposed to carry ten men. T here are fifteen hun- dred men waiting upon the river banks. All this may end in a perfect fizzle, a few may get hurt; and it may come to an apparent sudden death.</p>
        <p>At the headquarters of the two politicl parties, Democrats and Repubiicans, slight notice is given to the movement,and a con tiruptuous tone is adopted when speaking of it, but under all this there is most certainly a very uneasy feeling about it, all over the country. Tnese armies are not, in a sense representative, and yet it would be a mistake to suppose they have no sympathisers throughout the country. Twelve months keen distress is causing thousands, it would be no exaggeration to say mil- lions of men and women to look around with an inquiring eye for the reason of this distress. We have had no fainine,axid se far as I can see, no blame can be laid on the workmen. Nature has done her share towards making the people prosperous, but strange to say, the very abundance the earth has given forth,seenis to have turned into a curse. Now what is the cause of it all ? It is wondrous strange that we should have a trade panic here in America which is supposed to trace its origin in the money panic that occurred in the Argentine Republic some years ago, and involved the Baring Bros.of London. Passing on to Australia, England, and India, the panic seems to have Jumped itself into the States, to have found everything favourable for its recep- tion. As malaria breeds and spreads under favour- able conditions, so does this frightful nightmare seem to be growing and growing with the corrupt conditions of life to which I have had so often to refer, since I came to the States. With the excep- tion of rent, and vegetables of the simplest kind, everything is to be had as cheap as in England, or in other words bread, fruit, clothing, and shoes, are to be had at reasonable prices. Of course the cloth- ing is pretty well all slop work, and the slices are , all factory made. So, with an abundance altogether beyond all past experience, poverty and distress were never before so acute, nay chronic in the United States as they are now. Mills, warehouses, and shops are teeming with everything that man requires,and yet we have the phenomena of armies of .tramps besieging the legislative halls begging for work or food. I do not care to introduce the word Socialism into all and , everything I may have to say, because I feel the word is very inadequate in comprehension. It does not to my mind focus history to the mind of the student so that he may judge of the future by a careful analysis of the past and present. Let this be as it may, I am certainly of those who believe that the various periods of distress which the manu- facturing world has seen since the American Civil ! War, are due" to permanent forces inherent in the very nature of our industrial eystem, and that the men now out of work are a part of the regular army of the unemployed." lam sure a great many ot our best men and women believe this to be so, but are afraid to acknowledge it, dreading to apply the only cure possible to the disease. All over the country, societies are being formed for discussion sud inquiry. Parting from the old system which we think we know well, to one of which we have no knowledge whatever, we have certainly no pre- cedents to guide us, requires care if we.would not bring about a state of things worse than the pre- sent. A cheering sign is that the one journal that freely opens its columns to the free discussion of this subject is the one journal during these hard times to increase its circulation, I refer to the Arena."</p>
        <p>In New York, during the past winter, it was urged upon the municipal authorities that money should be raised by taxation and the issuing of bonds to find work for the unemployed. Against this it ss as contended that "poverty would have to be taxed to provide such work for the poor." This was taken, and rightly so, as a public acknowledgment that " the incidence of taxation falls heaviest upon men in proportion as they are unable to pay." It is an undoubted fact that the poor man pays, relatively, at least fifty per cent. more for his two, or three miserable tenement rooms, for his three or four pounds of coal, for his few pounds of bread, for his few ounces of tea and so on,than does the rich man. Let the poor man once get the Old Man of the Sea squarely astride his shoulders, and he may cast all hope on one side. The nobility of France brought about the French Revolution through saddling the poor with all the taxes. I should not be surprised any morning to find the American people in arms and civil war spreading through the land. I might say that with care such a disaster could not come about, but who and what are the men that can exercise this case. A very few names could be told off the owners of which are in the Legislation Halls from patriotic motives. In the Senate very few, very few indeed, are the members who could honestly take oath that they were not there to look after their own interests. It is either the railway interests, the sugar trust, the standard oil trust, or some business likely to be influenced by the new tariff laws that is surely re- ceiving their undivided attention. I could'nt begin to enumerate even a part of the long list of special interests these men are in Congress and Senate for. To look after the interest of the nation in a patrio- tic and unselfish fashion is about the last thing these men seem to think of. " Boodle " is the god they worship, whether it be in the municipal hall, dr the State legislature at Washington. How then *re these men likely to deal with the unemployed question? Mentally they are, as a body totally unfit to deal with a problem, that ',tends second to none, that the world has been called upon to solve. If I were to judge of the results of paying mem- bers from what I see here, I should pray my Radical friends at home to cast this feature out of their program. Caricatures in some of the papers place the position before the people in the best light possible. " Harper's Weekly" pictures the original " Coxey Army "as one arriving at Washington in Pullman cars. Carnegie heads the troop with peti- tion in band labelled ," Help the feeble steel indus- try." Others follow, " Help a poor sugar refiner, " Assist the poor tin plate infant," Help the wool interest," and so on, going the round of the indus- tries. " Feed our infant enterprises," is the rally- ing cry for this the original Coxey Army. Carnegie's firm has recently been found guilty of passing upon the Government steel plates for which it had been paid a premium over atd sb ive the contract. 'mice. For this he had to retuisi a large sum of money £16,000. Fancy the British manufacturers of cuffs and collars having locate in the House of Commons men whose known object was to influence legislation in their favour, threat- ening to discharge their workmen unless they signed petitions to influence legislation. Yet this is all being done in this glorious land of liberty. No doubt my readers will think, after resdin.; the above, that lamin a bad way. Nothit gof the sort, I simply think that the worst thing we can do is to look at everything from a blind uotiruistic point of view, ignoring the coming avalanche until we are overwhelmed. In Ohio the militia were called out to quell a riot. For obeying the call of the Governor the militia, who are workmen in the</p>
        <p>_ various factories were discharged by their em- ployers, who, evidently, would much rather hare had the riot go on, as being likely to intimidate the Senate in its action with the tariff measures. In the meantime the Governor threatens to prosecute the employers for intimidating the militia.</p>
