      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PROPOSED IMPROVEMENTS iN WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED IMPROVEMENTS iN WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>INFORMAL MEETING OF RATEPAYERS..</p>
        <p>On- Thursday last an informal meeting of a number of large ratepayers of Warrington was held tor the purpose of considering the articles which have appeared in the columns of the "Examiner" during the past nine weeks,on the question of muni- cipal finance and town improvements. The meet- ing was held at the residence of one of the party, and an " EXaMiller" reporter was invited to attend. It may be said that the meeting was representa- tive of a small ratepayers association which has been informally started in the town, and which it is intended to organize in the customary way before long. Some of the more ardent minded of thnse present expressed the opinion that the ratepayers of War- rington were not having their interests adequately baked after by the members of the Town Council, but it was agreed that, further action should be deferred until November next, when steps would be ' taken to obtain the pledges of candidates tor seats on the Council to resolutions similar to the follow- ing: (1) If elected will you undertake at the earliest possible moment, to give notice of motion at a Council meeting, or support a resolution if brought forward that an Act of Parliament be obtained for the purpose of consolidating the various loans hither- to obtained by the Warrington Corporation. (2) Will you vigorously and strenuously advocate the necessity of obtaining a period of not less than GO years tor the repayment of stock to be issued in repayment of the loans. (3) Will you advocate the absolute necessity of including in the proposed Act of Parliament a clause for the widening of the four main streets, viz : San- key-st., Bridge-st., Hersemarket-st., and Butter- market-st. _</p>
        <p>(4) Will you advocate that powers be obtained for the erection of buildings of not less than lour stories in height, in the four main streets men- tioned. - -</p>
        <p>_ (5) Will you advocate that power be obtained for the construction by the Corporation of a cable road.</p>
        <p>(6) Will you advocate that immediate steps be taken to bring about the construction of the War- rington docks, either with the co-operation of the Ship Canal Company, or by obtaining money for their construction by the Warrington Corporation. It was further resolved that in October next a series of meetings should be organized throughout Warrington to which candidates should be invited with a view to receiving a distinct undertaking that they will use all the power that in them lie. to bring about a. better state of affairs than present exists in Warrington, and to abolish as lac as possible the party cliques which it is considered often prevent amicable adjustments on questions_of importance. It was further resolved that the sec- retary pro tern be instructed to make arrangewents for the publication in pamphlet form of the series of interviews on municipal questions which have been puhlislied in the "Examiner,"and that the said pamphlets be distributed among the ratepayers of the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>" KLEPTOMANIAC " LANDLO lID.</head>
        <p>A " KLEPTOMANIAC " LANDLO lID.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. F. Wilson, a landowner, of The Gale. Cumber- land, and a magistrate for the county, was fined £lO and costs at Dutntries on Monday for stealing six Wedg- wood ware plates, two china cups, and a china saucer. A sale took place at Solway Cottage, Airman, on the Itl of May, and the articles were stolen by the defendant while making an inspection prior to te sale. Forthe defrnce it was urged that the defendant was a kleptc- n-.aniac, and it was stated tlia% atter he was charged with thi: offence he showed to his frit nds alarge number of comparaively worthless articles whicn he had stolen at other sales and secreted in his house. The Sheriff, however, said he k •lieved that Mr. Wilson was perfectly well aware of what he was doing when he stole the articles which formed the subject oi the chug°.</p>
      </div>
