        <p>GRAVING DOCK QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—With regard to the report issued in the columns of your paper, re conference held in the Town Hall, Run- corn. between Commissioners and deputation of workingmen, re-graving docks at Runcorn, we, the undersigned, on behalf of the deputation disclaim having authorised any person to furnish you with that report. —Signed,</p>
        <p>J. W. BOYLE, 31, Regent-st. A. RA.VENSCROFT, 19, Egerton-st., Runcorn. June 13, 1894.</p>
        <p>THE BREWER AND THE PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Seeing the other week in your valuable paper two cases against Warrington publicans, leads me to believe that the magistrates are about to stop these law breakers. But why not go to the root of the mischief? Say, nest Brewster Sessions, let every publican and off- licence holder appear personally before the magistrates, and kt them inquire into the conditions in which they are engaged, whether bona-fide tenants or bogus tenants —one of the Army of Removables, the bogus manager system. The latter is the curse of the trade. The big brewer puts the manager in and finds him a chucker out, paying the manager a small wage so that they can sup- ply what is called the " long pull." Here is where the mischief lies. The bona-fide tenant cannot compete with the long pull so he runs the risk of breaking the law, as the tenants did the other week. • The brewer is very careful which houses he selects for the manager system—all the best so that he can mono- polise all the trade. But the houses hecannot make a big profit, on he let off to bring in rent, and the poor tenant runs the risk of breaking the law everyday to try and get a living. Let the magistrates take an example from the Crewe magistrates and refuse every publica.n that cannot show that he is a bona-fide and not a bogus tenant. It is a fraud on the magistrates who grant a licence to a man who has to hand it ever to the brewer as soon as he gets it.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>A LOVER OF JUSTICE.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN IMPROVEMENT COMMIS- SIONERS. ANNUAL MEETING: ELECrION OF CHAIRMAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>STOPPAGE OF PASSENGER STEAMERS ON THE SHIP CANAL AT RUN CORN.</head>
        <p>THE STOPPAGE OF PASSENGER STEAMERS ON THE SHIP CANAL AT RUN CORN.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Commissioners for the purpose of appointing a Chairman was held on Wednesday merning in the Board-room, Town Hall, Runcorn. There were present Messrs. S. Taylor, ex-chairman, E. Imison,J. W. Lightburn,Wm. Hand- ley, H. J. Grice, J. Pritchard, D. Bisbrown, Wm Leach, and F. Wiswall, with the law clerk, Mr. F A. Lake, and the secretary, Mr. E. Marshall. The Clerk said that in recent years it had been the custom for the ex-chairman to take the chair at the Annual meetings. As the chair was at present vacant he accordingly called upon Mr. S. Taylor to fill it if he bad no objection.—Other members having expressed the wish that Mr. Taylor should preside he did so, saying that he gladly acquiesed if it was the wish of the whole Baa.re. He then went on to explain that the meeting ,had been called to appoint a chairman who would occupy- the position until November, when the Local Govern- men Act Act would come into operation. Mr. Wiswall proposed that under present circum- stances,l,./Ir. S. Taylor be re-elected Chairman for the few remaining months which the Board would exist. Mr. Bisbrown in seconding said he was personally op- posed to a member holding the chairmanship for more- than one year, but seeing that the Board would cease to, exist in November, and in view of the general satisfac- tion with which Mr. Taylor had always performed his duties, he felt it a pleasure to second his re-election. Mr. Imison thought that now that an opportunity presented itself they should elect as cl:airman one of their older members who had, as yet,not had the honour of presiding over their meetings. Mr. Taylor had had that pleasure for one year, and he (Mr. Imison) felt sure that Mr. Taylor would not now be so intensely selfish as to be wishful to retain the position. Mr. Pritchard was an old member of the Board, and had previously bad the post offered to him but had declined it. Now, however, he would most probably like to have his name enrolled amongst their list of chairmen. Mr. Imison went on to suggest that the Board resolve itself into Committee for the purpose of fully discussing the mat- ter. Mr. Lightburn, while he agreed with the general pt-in ciple embodied in the proposition of Mr. Imison namely, that any other member shoeld have an oppor- tunity of becoming Chairman of that Commission, thought that it would be unwise to make any change. It would only last for a very short time, and it might just as well die a natural death under the present arrangement. Mr. Pritchard remarked that he had attended the meeting with the view of supporting the proposition to replace Mr. Taylor in the chair. It was very kind of Mr. Imison to mention his name, but he could not think of monopolising both the chair of the School Board and that of the Commissioners. He supported the motion that Mr. Taylor retain the office. Mr. Taylor while admitting that it would be a pleasure to occupy the chair again, said that if Mr. Pritchard would accept it, and Mr. Handley wojild move a resolu- tion to that effect, he would most gladly second it. He (Mr. Taylor) felt sure that Mr. Pritchard would make a good chairman, he having had considerable experience in the work of that Beard. Mr. Leach and Mr. Grice both supported the re- election of Mr. Taylor. admitting, at the same time, the qualifications of Mr. Pritchard for the position. Mr. Handley agreed with Mr. Imison that they should have absolete freedom in the selection of their chairmen, and he understood that they had met that merning on -open ground to appoint whom they liked. He did not think, however, that Mr. Imison wished to spoil the un- animity of the meeting. Mr. Imison: Certainly not. The motion on being put to the meeting was carried. Mr. Wiswall congratulated Mr. Taylor upon his re- election to the office, end expressed his conviction that he would discharge his duties in the future as he had done in the past. Mr. Imison also added his congratulations to the Chairman, saying, that the position he had taken up was -only the advocating of the principle arrived at by the Board in the past, namely, that another member should have an opportunity of possessing the position. The Chairman in accepting the chair said, he thanked them all very much for the honour they had shown him. The business in the past year had, he added, proceeded without a single hitch, good feeling and respect having existed amongst the members. THE STOPPAGE OF PASSENtiER STEAMERS AT RUNCORN. Mr. Pritchard spoke at length on the disadvantages under wnich intending passengers on steamers from and to Runcorn laboured through there not being any fixed times for such steamers to stop at Runcorn and throueh there not being a proper stage for landing such pas- sengers. With a view to remedying this state of affairs he moved "That the Board anp ,int a deputation to wait upon the Manchester Ship Canal Passeneer Steamer Company to request them to provide facilities for land- ing and embarking passengers at Runcorn." Several members supported the proposition, and cited the many advantages which would result both to the public and the Company if such a provision was made. `Under the present circumstances, people who wist:ed to sail from one point to the other had to go very often by train to Eastham or Manchester. Mr. Pritchard suggested a. a very favourable site for the landine-stage that part of the embankment near the old ferryslip. Ultimately it was decided that Mr. Taylor and Mr. 'Speakman (Impose the deputation, and that the Com- pany should be waited upon at the earliest possible opportunity.</p>
        <p>WORTH E.NOWING.—WOODEND'S FAMOUS NINE OILS. is a boon to sufferers A i 4 Lets like magic in all ca,s .of RH HUMATISM, SPRATNS. BRUISES, and STIFF JOINTS. Prioe Is. 60. rer bottle. earl-law. ps.id.—J.WouDENr. -4:the/vlst. 22. Pitt-etreet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
