      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET. Warrington had the unexpected luxury of chronicling their first victory for the season on Saturday, and this over the powerful Sefton team. They made no mist.ikt about it either, for they won by 110 to 76. Now that they have got into the winning vein, it is to be hoped that they will keep there to the end of the season. George Clarke was the captain of the Warrington team, and I don't know whether it was his genial spirit which put courage into the Warrington players, but however that may be, he is to be congratulated on having that post on the occasion of Warrington's first victory. Al- though the season is now so well advanced, Warrington have not suffered the indignity of being thrashed by any team twice over, so that they have yet plenty of oppor- tunities of revenge.</p>
        <p>The cycling sports took a great number away from the match, but still there was a good attendance. Sefton were very fortunate in winning the toss, as there was an excellent wicket. The Sefton players appeared to like Bates' bowling, for he was unable to get a single wicket, and the wickets were shared by G. Clarke, Connor and Bibby. Clarke,of course, is almost always of service with the ball,and Connor is becoming an excellent bowler, for he captured four of the Sefton wickets at the small cost of laruns. Bibby's analysis however is a wonderful one, for he had only 4.2 overs. Three of these were maidens, and he managed to capture three wickets for a solitary run. Bibby has surely justified his inclusion in the first eleven, and he will no doubt ere long, be as useful a member of this team as he was of the second. The Sefton batsmen were a very even lot. Handford was the highest scorer with 16. Half the team attained double figures, but as it will be seen, nothing sensational was done by any of them.</p>
        <p>The score of 76 was not a very high one to surpass, and with Wordsworth and Bates in,there was a prospect of a merry time of it. Wordsworth, who has made some very good scores recently, however did not come off at all on Saturday, as he was caught before he had a single run. S. M. Crosfield filled up the breach, and he appeared to be bent on giving the Sefton fielders as much trouble as he could, for he knocked the ball to the boundary time after time. At length when he had scored 28, he ran out to a hall from the erstwhile Lancashire county bowler, Oakley, and slipped, and his bails were whipped off in a twinkling. The departure I of S. M. Crosfield appeared to have upset the Warrington players, for the next three batsmen each made a "duck," so that things began to look bad. Clark, the Warrington wicket keeper, now came to the help of his side, but it was soon Bates' turn to go back to the pavilion. None of the remaining Warrington players did anything brilliant with the bat, but left all the scoring to Clark who was proof against all the wiles of the Sefton bowlers, and carried his bat out for 35, which was the high2st score of the day.</p>
        <p>Clark is without a doubt one of the best all round men in the Warrington team, for he can bat and bowl as well as keep wicket. He came in at'a very critical time for Warrington on Saturday, for when three goocii men are dismissed without scoring, it is apt to make the succeeding players rather nervous. Clark however succeeded in practically pulling the match out of the fire. This victory will do Warrington a lot of good. It must be disheartening to a team, to be dogged with mis- fortune at every match they play, and now that they have been blessed with a change of fortune, they will have more confidence in themselves. The bowling and the fielding of the team are very good, in fact above the average. It is in the run getting, that Warrington have failed most frequently. This department has improved considerably during the last few weeks, and should this improvement continue, there will be more victories to chronicle for our local club.</p>
        <p>The second team was not so fortunatP as their seniors, and Dame Fortukte who smiled so kindly upon them at the commencement of the season, appears to be trans- ferring her favours to the first team. The WarrinE,,ton team were short-handed, and had to employ a sub. They made 77 in their innings, a notable feature of which was that Adamson who went in first carried his bat ri2ht through the innings for 34. Sefton replied with 188 for eight wickets, so that they inflicted a crushing defeat on their visitors. The Sankey Wire Mills second proved too good for the St. Mary's team on Saturday for they were 22 runs ahead of them at the close of the game, with a wicket to fall. Moss, Crosskill, and Kenwright, with 15, 11, and 11 not out respectively were the best of the Wire men, and McCue with 12 relieved the monotony of the S. Mary's</p>
        <p>sc,ring. --- Cairo-st. Unity paid a visit to Aston Flail on Saturday and returned defeated by 13 runs. Tnis result was brought about by Carpenter, who made 23. Noae of the remaining Astonians could get higher than five, so that it was a victory for Carpenter far more than for Aston.— Buckley-at. were also among the defeated teams on Saturday, receiving their quietus from Widnes Red Rose. Gibstm worked hart to alter the result for after bein; the highest scorer on his side with 14. he succeeded in capturing seven of the Red Rose wickets.</p>
        <p>The team with the solemn and funereal narae of Cemetery White Rose, fell victims to the Fairfield team on SaturdaY, and could not get within 13 runs of them. Better luck next time.—Two junior teams for the opposite sides of Warrington also fell foul of each other, viz. Wycliffe and Whitecross, and Wycliffe, although on their own ground, were beaten by 53 to 24, Mr." E.lnras " was a very hir.fh scorer for Whitc3cross, so that Wycliffe had better look to their fielding a little better.</p>
        <p>A match was played on Saturday between the local cricket team belonging to Messrs. Feter Walker and Sons, and the Chester agency of the same firm. The match was a very even one, and the Chester men only won by three runs. The figure nought will be seen v:.ry frequently in the scores of the teams. The Warrington team has the majority, no fewer thaimight ducks falling to their lot. Chester is not far behind for they have six. Most of the Wavers were clear howled, el that the bails had a bad time of it in the match. The second team of the Warrington club were more fortunate, for they defeatPd the Grammar School by 63 to 63. J. Lloyd played well for 28, and E. F. Guigh closely fol- lowed him on the Grammar School side with 20.</p>
        <p>The Penketh Friends proved too good for the Sankey Wire Mills on Saturday, although the latter were on their own ground. Reynolds was to a great exte•At responsible for this result for he scored 54 in fine style. This player consequently made more than half the Friends score, which was 96. Sankey Wire Mills replied with 64, and had the last batsman continued two min- utes lorrier at the wickets, he would have made the game a draw, for only two minutes remained to play. The Friends have done very well so far this season with local clubs, as they have only suffered defeat once thus far, their conquerors being Sankey Wesleyans who have also drawn with them.</p>
        <p>An extraordinary bowling performance was done by P. Johnson, on Saturday, for the Co-operative Stores against Penketh Wesleyan,. His analysis was eight wickets for no runs. Notwithstandint; this feat, how- ever, the Wesleyans won by 23 to 18, which does not show very well for the batting of the •extployes of the Co-operative Stores.-</p>
        <p>The Cheshire Lines Eng.ineers defeated the Mi.nchester Y.M.C.A. by 45 to 22 on Saturday. The Engineers have had a very good record up to date, for they have won Nur, tied one with the Bre werv,and lost crie to Buxton. In the match on Stturday Harrison took four of the "young men's" wickets for six runs, and Monks six for 15. Harrison was also the highest scorer with 15 not out to his credit, and he was closely followed by Brown with 14. On the other side, Coates scored almost a half of the total score of the Y.M.C.A.,his contribution being ten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>RETURN OF MR. C. E. PARKER.—On Saturday last Mr. Parker returned from his btlSin,A9 trip to California. He arrived at the station about four, ween he was met by a body of the workmen from the tannery who headed by the band playing " Home Sweet Home " walked up to his house where they dispersed. NEW BANNER FOR THE MISSION. —ln the Mission room, Penketh, is now on view a splendid new banner which has been subscribed for by the Men's Sunday afternoon -Bible CIASS. It is of purple brocade bordered with gold silk and bearing on one side a.hand-painted centrepiece representing the mission, while on the reverse side is a painting of the flight of Joseph and Miry into Egypt. The banner will he used on Walking Day, and will be a very striking object in the procession. TEA MEETING.—On Monday evening a tea meeting was held in the Schoolroom, Penketh, in celebration of Mr. Parker's return. After a substantial tea, a IllePtiEg was held at which short addreAsei were given by James Dodds,Esq., and others. Mr. Parker then described the varied scenes he had passed through during his trip to America. After the usual votes of thanks the meeting dispersed, thoroughly pleased with the evening's enter- tainment. Sr ccEss?tm LOCAL ATHLETES.—On Saturday lagt,Mr. B. Newcomb succeeded in ,tiriing a sp4ndid gold wech, being the Ist prize in th- members race at the Warring- ton C.C. sports. Mr. T. Hearns also gained the nnri prize in the same race, the prize being a set of carvers in case. Mr. W. Jacksoi, of the Crov n and Cashion, got the 2nd prize, a silver Present watch, in the two miles open handicap in the same sports.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 23, 1894. 94TH ANNIVERSARY OF THE BRUNS- THE SHIP CANAL. WICK SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JUNE 23, 1894. 94TH ANNIVERSARY OF THE BRUNS- THE SHIP CANAL. WICK SUNDAY SCHOOL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>THE HISTORY OF THE SCHOOL. The anniversary services in connection with the Brunswick Wesleyan Sabbath School, were held on Sunday last in the St. Paul's Wesleyan Chapel, Run- corn. There were large congregations both morn- ing and evening, the pulpit being occupied on each occasion by the Rev. W. L. Watkinsou, of London. Appropriate hymns and pieces were sung by the choir and scholars, and the services were much enjoyed. On Monday evening, a public meeting was held in the chapel, Mr. J. B. Leach, J.P., of Huyton, pre4ding. There were present on the platform, which had been specially erected for the occasion, the Revs. W. L. Watkinson, T. Wilkes, Wrn. Arrowsmith and J. Hanby, Messrs. F. J. Norman, J. P., A. R. Norman, R. Rawlinson, J. M. Frith, John Simpson, Wm. Pope, Sza. The Chairman said he felt very greatly the honour of presiding over that gathering, and although he did not like to occupy the position at first, thinking that some- one more capable might have been fou ad, yet had he not been present he should have felt very unhappy and uncomfortable. He hoped they would pardon him when he said that if there was one place he liked better than another it was the obi Brunswick Sunday School. (Applause.) It was just 50 years ago since it had been his privilege to enter that school, at which time he was probably carried into it, and put into the infants' class. He should never forget the connection— (hear, hear)—for he believed that the Sunday School had proved a personal blessing to him throughout the 50 years of his life. (Hear' hear.) Runcorn would not have been what it was that day had it not been one of the first towns that supported the Sunday Scheol movement when first inaugurated. One hundred years ago the Sunday School movement commenced here, Chester being one of the first towns, he thought, which possessed a school. That was in the year 1784. Soon after, one was established in Runcorn and what a fine array of men they had had connected with the movement in Runcorn; men who had made good and lasting impres- sions upon the minds of the young people who had been under their tuition. (Applause.) The Sunday School movement-perineated society to such an extent that the whole of our national life was influenced by it : and had it not been far the establishment of the schools, the con- dition of things would have been vastly different to what they were at present. The schools had supplied the sinews of war for the Church, and to-day their churches abounded with scholars who held various posi- tions therein. The schools first started with the Wes- leyan Methodists. In 1726 one of the first schools was inaugurated, and in 1737 John Wesley went to America for the purpose of establishing them, or to assist in the movement. In 1783 John Wesley addressed a large meeting of children in Runcorn, which was before the modern Sunday Schools had got a footing at all. About 1788 the Sunday school movement com- menced in Runcorn so that he thought he was right in saying that the movement began with their great leader John Wesley. (Applause.) Subsequently, Robert Raikes came into prominence and the work- went on until 1803 when that grand institution, the London Sunday School Union, was established, which had done so much good work throughout the world. They had had identified with them the leading men of the time. The greatest men claimed to have been either Sunday school teacners or scholars, and the same conditions of things existed at present, only on a much larger scale. (Hear, hear.) To- day the Sunday school movement stood to the front as one of the greatest of which God was to be thanked. (Ap- plause). According to the statistics of 1889 there were 1.996,605 teachers; and 17,000,720 scholars in their Sunday schools. These figures, however, had since gradually grown, and the numbers at the present tim e would prove interesting. (Applause.) Mr. John Simpson, who was received with loud ap- plause, referred to the good services rendered to the cause by Mr. J. B. Leach who he was pleased to see occupying • the chair. To give a full history of 94 1/ years of Brunswick school was of course a hopeless task but he would do the best he could. As moat of them knew the school was established in 1800, but he had never be able to make out who was the chief means of establishing it. He had always understood, however, that Mr. Thomas Hazlehurst, the father of the present Mr. Thomas flaztehurst, had had a great deal to do with the intro- duction of Methodism in Runcorn at the end of the last century, and he thought that that gentleman had alsa a great deal to do with the establishment of the Bruns- wick Sunday school. Good old Win. Holt—(loud ap- plause)—used to tell him that sometimes he was superin- tendent, secretary, and all the teachers. (Laughter.) The school used to be carried on in a cottage which stood where the goods station was now situated. Win. Holt began his work in connection with the school in the early part of the present century, and continued it until his death in 1863, dying at the age of GO years. His grand-children and great grand-children were brought up amongst them, and some of the latter were making themselves useful now in the school. His great-great- grand-son was also one of their juvenile scholars. He (the speaker) wished that others would follow these examples and take a similar interest in their school. Early in the present century the school was removed from the room in the Holloway to a room in Nelson-st. where it carried on until the Brunswick Chapel was erected. Then the school was again removed and this time carried on beneath the Chapel, until the building in which they now were, was presented to them. The school was then brought up from the cellar of Brunswick, and conducted in the chapel where it had remained ever since. The school was still flourishing. They had had " ups and downs "of course, but it was conducted with more or less success. At the present time they had four schools, the number of scholars attending them being 1,636, who were taught by 147 teachers. These were kept going at the cost of £2OO per year. The scholars at the Brunswick school numbered 533, of whom 170 were upwards of 15 rears of age. There were at the school 72 teachers arsi lie was glad to say they possessed a flourishing Band of Hope, the membership of which was 450. (Applause.) Last year they raised £B3 for the Foreign Missionary Society in the Brunswick School alone. (Hear, hear.). He thought that their success within tha last forty years was mainly due to the hard work of Mr. Sinister, who :was now superitatendent,and to their having separate class-rooms. The children were taught so much better in separate rooms, as the traeher was better able to get the more concentrated attention of his or her scholars. Mr. Simpson then went on at considerable length to mention the names of those ladies and gentlemen who had assisted them in the past, and who were assisting them now and concluded by citing the many beneficial effects which Sunday schools worked upon those who attended them. In a few words regard- ing his own connection with the school he said lie began working for the school in 1833 and was gradually advanced from one class to another. He acted as school secretary and in 1348 was chosen one of the superin- tendants. Later he succeeded Mr. J. Potter as treasurer of the Sunday school Sick Club. His colleagues had worked very harmoniously with him, and now that he could not do as much as he used to, they very kindly made up for his shortcomings. (Hear, hear.) About 6.5 years ago his father took him to the school and he attrie buted his success in life to his connection with it. He felt it an honour to be connected with such a school ,and he thought that the least he could do in return for the blessings lie had received Irons it, was that as long as God should enable him he would devote his energies for- the welfare of the school. Ile hoped it would prosper ' more and more in the future than in the past. (Hear hear, and applause.) 'rho Rev. W. L. Watkinson, who was heartily wel- comed, expressed himself as very pleased to meet them together that evening, and congratulated them upon , possessing such a spacious and mag,niticent shrine in which to worship God. It had always done him good to see this chapel; and to be present at the anniversary was delightful. In the course of his subsequent remai ks, Mr. Watkinson said—all the problems of society were to be solved by the consideration of the children. Allthei problems were in a nutshell, and thatshell was the skill of the scholar or child. And all those questions which perplexed men to-day would be solved, not by Parlia mentary dealing with adults, but by the schoolmaster dealing with the children ; and in the more perfect edu- cation of the children's minds would come that better and nobler state for which we had slaved. (Eiear, hear and applause.) The Rev. Wilkes proposed that a hearty vote of thanks be given to the speakers, choir, and scholars, for the parts they had taken in the anniversary pro- ceedings. The thanks were unanimously accorded. The Chairman observed that the result of the collec- tions had just been made known to him, and he was very pleased to inform them that for the total services, Ste., they had realised £72 10s., being an advance on last year's offertories of £7 103. It was the largest amount they had subscribed at any of their previous anniversaries. (Loud applause.)</p>
        <p>A KNIGHTHOOD FOR MR. LEADER WILLIAMS. The Prime Minister has intimated to Mr. Leader , Williams, the engineer of the Manchester Ship Canal, ' that Her Majesty has conferred the honour of knight- hood upon him. Mr. Edward Leader Williams is a member of the In- stitute of Civil Engineers and of the institute of Mecha- nical Engineers. He is the eldest son of the late engi- neers of the Sevetn Navigation, and received on those works his early professional training., 1844-1850. He was then engaged as a resident engineer in the con- struction of the Great Northern Railway. Mr. Williams was appointed in 1856 engineer to the Weaver Trust,and most of the important works on that river are associa- ted with his name—notably the Anderton Lift, connect- ing the Trent and Mersey Canal with the Weaver Navi- gation and rectifying a difference in the water level of 52ft. In this a,chivetnent was foreshadowed the Barton Aqueduct. Before completely his work on the Weaver Mr. Williams received the appointment of engineer to the Bridgewater Navigation Company, and the enlarge- ment of the Runcorn Docks was carried out under his direction. On the 26th September, 1882, his plans for the Ship Canal to Manchester were unanimously adopted by the Provisional Committee. From that moment he became a marked man, and his many friends, both lay and professional, will be gratified to -learn that the dis- tinction he has earned amongst them is to receive a Royal endorsement. The hopefulness and the presever- ance, at once dogged and genial, of Mr. Williams has often stood him in good stead at critical moments during the last six years. The great work which he has never stood aside from nor loosed his hold of, and which crowns his life, includes forty distinct items, each of which is an undertaking, apart from a host of smaller ones which would all have been large in any other con- nection. The great' possibility is now with us, struc- turally complete, admitted on all hands to be well and truly done. THE WORK AT WALTON LOCK.</p>
        <p>The work at Walton lock is steadily progressing, al- though during the past few weeks the men have encoun- tered great difficulties from the river which has broken in several times. The lock gates are now practically finished, and in about a month the sluices will also be complete unless something unforeseen occurs. There are a great numbe*f men still employed on the works,but it is expected that in about three months' time the whole of the work will be accomplished. During the present ',reek a number of men have been busily en- gaged in cutting through the embankment between the Walton end of the old bed of the river and the small branch of the Ship Canal which will in some future date form the entrance to the Warrington docks. When this work is complete as it is expected to be this week, the tide which comes up the Ship Canal will also enter the old cutting, and to some extent clear it from the stag- nant water, and thus abate the nuisance which has been caused for so long a time in Wilderspool Causeway.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>MANCHESTER AND THE CANAL.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND THE CANAL.</p>
        <p>The adjourned debate on the affairs of the Ship Canal was resumed at a special sitting of the Manchester City Council on Wednesday. Most of the speakers took a sanguine view of the prospects of the undertaking, and a desire was expressed that the board of directors should be strengthened by the addition of one or two gentle- men of shipping experience. One speaker affirmed that taking all the circumstances into account, the conetruc- tion and opening of the canal constituted an engineering triumph which had not been transcended in the Victorian age. Not a word, he added, had been said of the prac- tical advantages which had been already reaped by the community, and Yet everybody knew that thousands of pounds had been saved since the scheme was projected. The Wholesale Co-operative Society invested £'20,000 in the canal, and it was believed that very nearly the whole of that amount had been already recovered. Alderman Clay, one of the cor- poration directors, strongly deprecatsd the state- mert made a fortnight ago by Sir John Harwood, and considered the council made a great mistake in coun- tenancing such a statement. That speech could only be described as extraordinary and cowardly, and it was made without the sanction or knowledge of the other corporation members of the directorate. He repudiated the idea that the old directors had kept back anything from the knowledge of the corporation. There was no reason, in his opinion, for taking a gloomy view of the future, and all that was needed was a serong and ex- perienced man at the head of affairs, and such a man he tholialit they had in Mr. Bythell. Ultimately the en- gineer's (Mr. Hill) report, on which Sir John B.ar wood's speech had been based, was unanimously adopted, and the council approved of the following resolution : "That the Corporation be authorised to create stock, to bs called the Manchester Corporation 1831 Redeemable Stock, and issue to an amount sufficient for raising the sum of £506,385 12s. which has not been raised on ac- count of the money, the Corporation is authorised by Parliament to raise under the powers conferred in them by the Manchester Ship Canal Act, 1861, as amended by the Manchester Corporatian Act, 1893." The price of stock was fixed at par, and the customary authority was given to the Finance Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MEErING OF SHAREHOLDERS.</head>
        <p>MEErING OF SHAREHOLDERS.</p>
        <p>An adjourned meeting of shareholders in the Ship Canal unelertaking was held on Thursday evening, Mr. Reuben Spencer again presiding. Cdnfidence in the ulti- mate success of the enterprize was expressed by all the speakers, and it was stated that several new develop- ments in the way of traffic were in progress. With re- gard to the benefit already enjoyed by the trading com- munity in consequence of the canal, Mr. Spencer did not hesitate to say that £500,000 did not represent it a all. That in itself was equivalent to more than two years's interest on the Corporation loan. THE COAL TRADE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>THREATENED SERIOUS STRIKE OF MINERS IN</head>
        <p>THREATENED SERIOUS STRIKE OF MINERS IN</p>
        <p>THE WIGAN DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>For some time past there has been a considerable amount of friction between the managers of the exten- sive collieries owned by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles and Co.. in the Wigan district, and the members of the Miners' Federation employed by them, who have brought forward a number of grievances which they have demanded shall receive satisfactory settlement. The matter was recently laid before the executive of the Miners' Federation, and with a view of having the wh,-le of these grievances settled in some, way or another they gave permission to the men to send in notices to cease work, the Federation undertaking to give them the usual financial support. These notices expired on Tues- day in this week, but Messrs. Pearson and Knowles, having placed their case before the Coalowners' Defence Association, and that Association having appointed a 'small committee to meet representatives of the Miners' Federation, a c,mmittee of the Federation has also been appointed to confer with the coalowners' committee, and in the meantime the notices have been postponed for a week with a view of possibly some amicable arrange- ment being come to. Unless some satisfactory settle- ment with the miners is arrived at, the strike will go on, and this will affect about 2,000 men employed at the various pits in the Wigan district owned by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles.</p>
      </div>
