      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Monday evening, Mr. 1. O'CoNNoa asked some further questions about recent; alleged irregularities in the Belfast whisky trade, The , firm concerned was Messrs. Dunville and Co. Sir W. HARCOURT said the fine paid was £5OO. The number altered or obliterated did not purport to represent tho age of the whisky, but the year of the last operation on it in the warehouse—that was to say, blending or rack- ing. There was no fraud or loss to the revenue. The right hon. gentleman, answering a question put by Mr. BARTLEY, said the date of the commencement of the operation of the Finance Bill would not be the 3li.st May, but some subsequent date to be hereafter fixed. Mr. CAINE drew attention to the new Manx Licensing Act and the powers it gives for the granting of licenses to boarding-house keepers, and asked the Home Secre- tary if he would advise the Queen to withhold her assent to it until after the next general election to the House of Keys. Mr. Asiturrn said the Act dealt with a matter of purely domestic concern within the sphere of the Manx Legislatures powers, and he did not prop,,se to interfere. . _ .</p>
        <p>The House again went into Committee on the Budget Bill. Amendments moved from the Opposition side wero defeated by majorities of 28 to 36.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords the Cambridge Corporation Bill (relating to the "Spinning-house ") was read a third time and passed.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Tuesday evening, Mr. ROBERTSON informed Mr.Kearley that it had been decided that the eight-hour day should come into operation at the naval establishments on the 2nd July. In reply to- a question put by Mr. Pierpoint about the storage of the flood waters of the Nile, Sir E. GREY said the report of the Commission appointed to examine the various schemes had not yet been made public. The estimated cost of a reservoir at Assouan and the works in connection with it was, according to that report, between five and six millions. The House again went into Committee on the Budget BM, and resumed consideration of clauses 7,8, and 9, which were added to the Bill.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords Viscount Cross moved tnat an address should be presented to the Queen praying Her- Majesty to withhold her assent from that part of the education scheme for the county of Denbigh which related to the Ruthin Grammar school. He contended that neither the Governors nor the Charity Commissioners had power to make any rules which would have the effect of changing the fundamental character of the school as a Church school. The Marquis of Salisbury supported the motion. The Earl of Rosebery and the Lord Chancellor held that the Privy Council,and not the House of Lords was the proper tribunal to decide this question. The Bishop of St. Asaph said want of funds prevented an appeal at the proper time to the Privy Council. The motion was carried 1.3777 to 19. The Bishop of St. Asaph had a motion on the paper relating to religious worship and teaching in boarding-houses and hotels in connection with education. Lord ROSE- /BELLY said that after the division en the previous motion he would not again divide the House, and the Bishop's motion was formally put and agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WEDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the Flkuse of Commons on. Wednesday,a motion for the recommittal of the London Streets and Buildings Bill was agreed to. On the vote of £27,715, for the salaries and expenses of the office of the Irish Chief Secretary, Mr. Arnold Forster moved its reduction by £2,000, in respect of the salary of the Chief Secretary. He complained that the right hon. gentleman had nottaken and was not taking adequate measures to prevent or suppress the practice of boycotting. Mr. T. W. Russell also criticised the Chief Secretary's action. Mr. W. Johnston, Mr. Webb, and others joined in the debate., and Mr. Plunkett said Mr. Morley had certainly done everything he could to promote the prosperity of the congested districts, and to make the policy of his predecessor in respect to those dist ricts a success. Mr. Jorrir REDMOND said he could not vote for the amendment, but he should not vote against it, because the Chief Secretary had been guilty of many grave mistakes in dispersing public meetings, interfering with freedom of speech, packing juries, and lending the forces of the Crown tor carrying out cruel and unjustifiable evictions. Mr. Morley vigorously defended his policy, and the proposed reduction was negatived by a majority of 39,_ The closure was carried by a majority of 50, and the entire vote was agreed to. The Intoxicating Liquor Local. Veto (Ireland) Bill was withdrawn. THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Thursday eveninr Sir W. flkacoußTrnformed Mr. J. Rowlands that the Official Liquidator of the Cardiff Savings Bank was now enraged in distributing the first and final dividend-3s. in the pound. The cost of the liquidation was expected to be about £9,600. In reply to Sir E. A. Bartlett,. Mr. BRITON said the Acting High Commissioner of South Africa had ad- dressed a friendly representation to the Government of the South African Republic on the subject of the com- mandeering of British subjects foriservice in the Trans- val. but no reply had yet been received. The House again went in Committee on the Budget Bill. The debate was adjourned. Just before thelionse rose Mr. T. W. Russell returned to the attack on the Chief Secretary for Ireland. Mr. MORLEY defended the statements he made en Wednes- day, and declared that there had been no winking; or conniving at boycotting and no compact with the Irish members. In the House of Lords,, on thn. motion of the Lord CHANCELLOR, the•bill to amend the Act for the Preven- tion of Cruelty to Children was read a second time. The noble Lord said that investigatiOn, had shown that the amount of cruelty to children daring the past ten years was appalling, and that the action of those who put the Provisions of the existing law in force had been most judicious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>OUR LOCAL. CHURCHES- W ARRINGTON</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL. CHURCHES- W ARRINGTON</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFF BAPTIST CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>In connection with this chapel Sunday school anniver- sary services were held on Sunday afternoon and even- ing. The Rev. F. Overend, of Bacup. was the preacher, and he delivered two excellent discourses to large con- gregations. Special hymns and anthems were string. The choir was under the conductorship of Mr. Benworthy, and the two anthems sung were "0 worship the Lord," and "The Lord preserveth the souls of His saints." Cel- lections were taken at both services on behalf of the church, school, and re-furnishing, funds. The satisfac tory amount of £5O was realised. , WYCLIFFE P.:S.A. SOCIETY. The weekly meeting of this society was held on Sun- day afternoon. Mr. David Owen occupied the chair,and in the course of a brief address he spoke from the text "What is man that thou art mindful of him," dwelling chiefly on the latter part of the text. Mr. G. J. Brown, delivered the address. lie based his remarks chiefly on the phrase often used by some men that they could look up from nature to nature's God. He admitted that it was perfectly true that they could do so, and that they ought to do, but his opinion was that they did not do so. He believed the phrase of looking up from nature to nature's God on the lips of the majority of men was nothing more than base phraseology, mere cant, and a subterfuge to evade just responsibility. God's gifts had made them so familiar with them that they forgot both</p>
        <p>he gift and the giver, and took them ass though theyt had a rightful claim to them. They acknlviledged tha 110 made them, hut as yet their knowledge only con- strained them to admire, when it ought to compel them ot worship. Mr. Fieldhouse was the soloist and sang as a son " Calvary." Messrs. T. Wallington, W. Beswick, and J. Ball also took part in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LAWN TE N MS.</head>
        <p>LAWN TE N MS.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-LB-WILLOWS v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Newton,who have now got together quite a respectable tennis club, played their first match since the formation of the club, on Saturday afternoon, their mpponents being Widnes. They were fortunately favoured with. splendid weather, and were able on the whole to send the Widnes players home defeated. The games were very well played, and in one or two instances were closely contested. The following are the scores : GENTLEMEN'S DOUBLES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Moncrief, Mr. Barinvv Widnes. beat Mr. Clarke. MY.. Anderton, Newton, 2-6-4-6 ; Mr. Carr, Mr. Glover, Widnes beat 'Mr. Latchford, Mr. Turhutt, Newton. 6-4-7-9-4 6.; Mr Longtan, Mr. Emmett, Newtan. beat Mr. Dohiggin,, Mr Hough, Widnes. 6-4-5-7-6-4; Mr. Liptrott, Mr. Sankey, Newton beat Mr. Tatters, Mr. White. Widnes, 6-3-6-4: Newton 5 sets, 52 games, , Widnes 5 sets, 53 games.• GENTLF.MEN'S SINGLES . NEWTON. WIDNES. Mr Otarke lost to Mr Morterief 2-6-6-4.-2-6 Mr Anderton beat Mr Barlow 6-4-5-2 Mr Latchford lost to Mr Carr 3-6-4-8 Mr Turbatt lost to Mr Glover 1-6-4-6 Mr Loagton bcat My Dobiggin 6-2-6-4 Mr Emmert) beat Mr Hough 6,3-6-2 Mr Lirrtrott beat Mr Tatters 6-3-8-6 Mr Sankey bat Mr White 6-0-6-4 Newton 11 sets 87 gAmes. Widnes 6 sets, 70 games. LAD/ E. 3 Dommr.s.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Parkes, Miss E. Liptrot, Newton, be 24 Miss Maddison, Miss B. M. Madclison„Wid,:es, ; Mi,a M. Liptrott, Miss E. 6 nkey, Newton, lost to Miss B. M. Maddison, Misa. Wilson, Widnes, 2-6-6-3-5-7; Miss A.Wond, Miss E. Urcinlme, Newton, beat Miss Barlow. Miss Forst-r. Widnes, 6-2-E-0. New' on 5 sets. 39 gAmes. Widnes 3 sets, 23 games. LAM&amp; SINGLE. 9.</p>
        <p>• wwwrox. Iv. DN ES. Mrs. Parkes beat Mies Mr ddison S-4.64 Ms 14. Li ptrott lost to Miss N. M. Maddison, 2-6-:i-0 Mi,s M. Liptrett bed, ;Vies R. M. Maticlisoa, 6.3-F-1 Miss N. Sankey beat Miss Wilson 3-6-11-9 Miss A. Wood beat Miss liirlovz 6-4-6-4 M. 1., aZ. Urquhart beat Miss Vorster 0-0-6-0 Newton 10 a •ti. 72 gamey, Wid,ned Z seo, 44 przies‘</p>
