      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-06-23_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>SWALLOWING 192 FLOORING NAILS,III</p>
        <p>LaU An inquest was held at the County Asylum, caster, on Wednesday, respecting the death of Vi'illiars Fitzpatrick, 44 years of age, which occurred on Tueri'. day night. The Coroner in opening the inquiry, eel" they were met to inquire into the death of a ma? Wh: swallowed a quantity of nails and other things (Pomtll3° to a basin half full of objectionable matter which the patient had swallowed). The wonder was how the patient succeeded in swallowing the things, and how Ir. had survived so long.—Dr. Gemmell,of the asylum eta gave evidence as to the deceased being broughb him. Be put him to bed, and found 2,, foreign body occupying the upper portion el the abdomen. After consultation he decided to operarlttse as there was a remote chance of saving his life- 1 operati6u was necessarDy tedious and dangerous, and' lasted a couple of hours. He found 192 flooring nalls°. varying from 3in. to IMn. in length, the smallest being atack. There was also a half-screw nail, two button,&amp; piece of wire, and a mass of matted hair. The operation was completed about seven o'clock. and the patient sank and died about eleven o'clocl: The cause of death wag' shock, conseqtlent on the operation. The mucous rarer brine of the stomach was lacerated by the nails.. Jelcei lother portions of the body were right. Tho nails weigh llb. 94-oz.—(sensation)—and must have been in the stomach 24 hours.—The jury returned a verdict of death from shock to the system.</p>
