        <p>SPECIAL PURCHASE oF BLACK SILKS, 25 TO 40 PER CENT. BELOW ORDINARY PRICES' The Fashion correspondent of a London paper says : each year there is a dominant note in Fashion; this year it is SILK. Since the early part, of May we have looked at. talked of, and written about English Silks until even the most unobservant have had it borne in on them that at last, if somewhat late in the day, we have discovered that we are neglecting one of our oldest British Indus- trles, and have now made up our minds to make silk, manufactured at home, really fashionable. That the recent National Silk Exhibition has already done good.it is easy to see. All our largest Silk Mercers now keep in Stock, and make up the products of English looms, and at really reasonable prices ; the qualit‘, of the materials being far better than the foreign cheap Silks with which this country has of late years been flooded. It is satis- factory to know that large orders have been placed with English Silk Weavers by the Queen, members of the Royal Family, and Ladies of high position in society. Now it id only for the multitude to follow the good example set, and English Silk will, by degrees, regain the foremost place in the market. Taffeta glace, of which there were many charming examples at the London Silk Exhibition, is admirably adapted for the all-silk gowns and blouses now so fashionable. Seeing that Silks are now so very fashionable, customers are respectfully solicited to avail themselves of this oppor- tunity of procuring a really serviceable, rich-looking. Black Silk Dress at the undermentioned low quotations : LOT 1, RICH MID SILK at, 2s. 11d., USUAL PRICE 3s. 11d. LOT 2, RICH CORD SILK at 3s. 11d., USUAL PRICE ss. 6d. LOT 3, RICH PEAU DE SOIE at 48. 11d., USUAL PRICE 6s. 6d. LOT 4, RICH PEAU DE SQIE at be. 6d., USUAL PRIOR 7s. 6d. E. JUAITANS, 17 To 23, MARKET STREET, EARLESTOWN ; AND 17, SANKEY-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
